{"char_offset": [1747, 3121], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:48:46Z", "heading_path": [], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 6, "paragraph_text": "Export RIS Citation Information. TY - A2 - National Center for Immunization and Respiratory Diseases (U.S.). Division of Bacterial Diseases. A2 - American Pharmacists Association. AB - Taking antibiotics for viral infections such as a cold, a cough, or the flu will not: AB - • Cure the infection AB - • Keep other people from catching it AB - • Help you feel better AB - Antibiotics are strong medicines. Keep them that way. Prevent antibiotic resistance. Antibiotics don’t fight viruses—they fight bacteria. Using antibiotics for viruses can put you at risk of getting a bacterial infection that is resistant to antibiotic treatment. Talk to your healthcare provider about antibiotics, visit www.cdc.gov/getsmart, or call 1-800-CDC-INFO to learn more. AN - cdc:29152 CY - Atlanta, GA KW - Anti-Bacterial Agents/therapeutic use KW - Antiviral Agents KW - Common Cold/drug therapy KW - Drug Resistance, Microbial KW - Influenza, Human/drug therapy KW - Anti-Bacterial Agents/therapeutic use KW - Antiviral Agents KW - Common Cold/drug therapy KW - Drug Resistance, Microbial KW - Influenza, Human/drug therapy LA - eng PY - 2008 T2 - Get smart : know when antibiotics work TI - Warning : Antibiotics don't work for viruses like colds and the flu. Using them for viruses will NOT make you feel better or get back to work faster UR - https://stacks.cdc.gov/view/cdc/29152 ER -", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "p[6]", "sha256_of_page": "aaadd56e9dcc4d7b4090b4e2d7bcdd09e692155d1cff941f36e843de34e14317", "sha256_of_paragraph": "11d15a91f23fb1affbb139f338256a0ec1c72d8f8ae3451dc6042c5a69369f35", "source_id": "CDC-STACK", "tag": "p", "url": "https://stacks.cdc.gov/view/cdc/29152"}
