{"char_offset": [214, 247], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:05Z", "heading_path": ["What Is Earth?", "Contents"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 6, "paragraph_text": "What Are Earth’s Different Parts?", "published_or_updated": "2026-11-18T13:24:25-05:00", "selector": "What Is Earth? > Contents > p[6]", "sha256_of_page": "2e3b5a2a0636dd0ce1284cf3817112c7268eb8e7a26259d26a696c1fa33f2644", "sha256_of_paragraph": "3f3b4419cb8e22ddb0759eca411b8930dc9d12b1ef1ca63366af32df8a6380f3", "source_id": "NASA-EARTH", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/learning-resources/for-kids-and-students/what-is-earth/"}
{"char_offset": [248, 282], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:05Z", "heading_path": ["What Is Earth?", "Contents"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 7, "paragraph_text": "Why and How Does NASA Study Earth?", "published_or_updated": "2026-11-18T13:24:25-05:00", "selector": "What Is Earth? > Contents > p[7]", "sha256_of_page": "2e3b5a2a0636dd0ce1284cf3817112c7268eb8e7a26259d26a696c1fa33f2644", "sha256_of_paragraph": "7cfe38e3583c0c7e57e52d96a6489932fd5db1599a11b51ef06d154784707413", "source_id": "NASA-EARTH", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/learning-resources/for-kids-and-students/what-is-earth/"}
{"char_offset": [283, 692], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:05Z", "heading_path": ["What Is Earth?", "Contents"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 8, "paragraph_text": "Earth is our home planet. Scientists believe Earth and its moon formed around the same time as the rest of the solar system. They think that was about 4.5 billion years ago. Earth is the fifth-largest planet in the solar system. Its diameter is about 8,000 miles. And Earth is the third-closest planet to the sun. Its average distance from the sun is about 93 million miles. Only Mercury and Venus are closer.", "published_or_updated": "2026-11-18T13:24:25-05:00", "selector": "What Is Earth? > Contents > p[8]", "sha256_of_page": "2e3b5a2a0636dd0ce1284cf3817112c7268eb8e7a26259d26a696c1fa33f2644", "sha256_of_paragraph": "925ab946348782b6fc9282e9bb1a2a1c07fcba2438bc132d9521b3cc5b261943", "source_id": "NASA-EARTH", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/learning-resources/for-kids-and-students/what-is-earth/"}
{"char_offset": [693, 1066], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:05Z", "heading_path": ["What Is Earth?", "Contents"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 9, "paragraph_text": "Earth has been called the “Goldilocks planet.” In the story of “Goldilocks and the Three Bears,” a little girl named Goldilocks liked everything just right. Her porridge couldn’t be too hot or too cold. And her bed couldn’t be too hard or too soft. On Earth, everything is just right for life to exist. It’s warm, but not too warm. And it has water, but not too much water.", "published_or_updated": "2026-11-18T13:24:25-05:00", "selector": "What Is Earth? > Contents > p[9]", "sha256_of_page": "2e3b5a2a0636dd0ce1284cf3817112c7268eb8e7a26259d26a696c1fa33f2644", "sha256_of_paragraph": "6a427bf07fe9e954365b083688052c9a0e9b439dd7f00a359550cb81c1b6db71", "source_id": "NASA-EARTH", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/learning-resources/for-kids-and-students/what-is-earth/"}
{"char_offset": [1067, 1227], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:05Z", "heading_path": ["What Is Earth?", "Contents"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 10, "paragraph_text": "Earth is the only planet known to have large amounts of liquid water. Liquid water is essential for life. Earth is the only planet where life is known to exist.", "published_or_updated": "2026-11-18T13:24:25-05:00", "selector": "What Is Earth? > Contents > p[10]", "sha256_of_page": "2e3b5a2a0636dd0ce1284cf3817112c7268eb8e7a26259d26a696c1fa33f2644", "sha256_of_paragraph": "2370567dfb237c0cb501ed10006dd342e45c5d6301621d431407df13a588a22e", "source_id": "NASA-EARTH", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/learning-resources/for-kids-and-students/what-is-earth/"}
{"char_offset": [1228, 1519], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:05Z", "heading_path": ["What Is Earth?", "What Does Earth Look Like?"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 11, "paragraph_text": "From space, Earth looks like a blue marble with white swirls and areas of brown, yellow, green and white. The blue is water, which covers about 71 percent of Earth’s surface. The white swirls are clouds. The areas of brown, yellow and green are land. And the areas of white are ice and snow.", "published_or_updated": "2026-11-18T13:24:25-05:00", "selector": "What Is Earth? > What Does Earth Look Like? > p[11]", "sha256_of_page": "2e3b5a2a0636dd0ce1284cf3817112c7268eb8e7a26259d26a696c1fa33f2644", "sha256_of_paragraph": "955a9b27624fbb238167eac1df95de9da5c08e90218f97c8623964436e78cd3e", "source_id": "NASA-EARTH", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/learning-resources/for-kids-and-students/what-is-earth/"}
{"char_offset": [1520, 1812], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:05Z", "heading_path": ["What Is Earth?", "What Does Earth Look Like?"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 12, "paragraph_text": "The equator is an imaginary circle that divides Earth into two halves. The northern half is called the Northern Hemisphere. The southern half is called the Southern Hemisphere. The northernmost point on Earth is called the North Pole. The southernmost point on Earth is called the South Pole.", "published_or_updated": "2026-11-18T13:24:25-05:00", "selector": "What Is Earth? > What Does Earth Look Like? > p[12]", "sha256_of_page": "2e3b5a2a0636dd0ce1284cf3817112c7268eb8e7a26259d26a696c1fa33f2644", "sha256_of_paragraph": "46fb97cfe69eadd399a6a394c260ffca8f519626b620d15363667a0f10aad3b7", "source_id": "NASA-EARTH", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/learning-resources/for-kids-and-students/what-is-earth/"}
{"char_offset": [1813, 2188], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:05Z", "heading_path": ["What Is Earth?", "How Do We Know Earth Is Round?"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 13, "paragraph_text": "Humans have known that Earth is round for more than 2,000 years! The ancient Greeks measured shadows during summer solstice and also calculated Earth’s circumference. They used positions of stars and constellations to estimate distances on Earth. They could even see the planet’s round shadow on the moon during a lunar eclipse. (We still can see this during lunar eclipses.)", "published_or_updated": "2026-11-18T13:24:25-05:00", "selector": "What Is Earth? > How Do We Know Earth Is Round? > p[13]", "sha256_of_page": "2e3b5a2a0636dd0ce1284cf3817112c7268eb8e7a26259d26a696c1fa33f2644", "sha256_of_paragraph": "a32e809646e309a07496eaf51ddcd5198d16bb96593548c215b12533d16912cd", "source_id": "NASA-EARTH", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/learning-resources/for-kids-and-students/what-is-earth/"}
{"char_offset": [2189, 2516], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:05Z", "heading_path": ["What Is Earth?", "How Do We Know Earth Is Round?"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 14, "paragraph_text": "Today, scientists use geodesy, which is the science of measuring Earth’s shape, gravity and rotation. Geodesy provides accurate measurements that show Earth is round. With GPS and other satellites, scientists can measure Earth’s size and shape to within a centimeter. Pictures from space also show Earth is round like the moon.", "published_or_updated": "2026-11-18T13:24:25-05:00", "selector": "What Is Earth? > How Do We Know Earth Is Round? > p[14]", "sha256_of_page": "2e3b5a2a0636dd0ce1284cf3817112c7268eb8e7a26259d26a696c1fa33f2644", "sha256_of_paragraph": "e6bfdef4251aca0cd593fb091ef942fd96260363c013aa2e9778d78304d2f024", "source_id": "NASA-EARTH", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/learning-resources/for-kids-and-students/what-is-earth/"}
{"char_offset": [2517, 2793], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:05Z", "heading_path": ["What Is Earth?", "How Do We Know Earth Is Round?"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 15, "paragraph_text": "Even though our planet is a sphere, it is not a perfect sphere. Because of the force caused when Earth rotates, the North and South Poles are slightly flat. Earth’s rotation, wobbly motion and other forces are making the planet change shape very slowly, but it is still round.", "published_or_updated": "2026-11-18T13:24:25-05:00", "selector": "What Is Earth? > How Do We Know Earth Is Round? > p[15]", "sha256_of_page": "2e3b5a2a0636dd0ce1284cf3817112c7268eb8e7a26259d26a696c1fa33f2644", "sha256_of_paragraph": "81a51caa4b1734e62df745f68dd09038153160220a7fce7cff7dc16c76941a59", "source_id": "NASA-EARTH", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/learning-resources/for-kids-and-students/what-is-earth/"}
{"char_offset": [2794, 3187], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:05Z", "heading_path": ["What Is Earth?", "How Does Earth Move?"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 16, "paragraph_text": "Earth orbits the sun once every 365 days, or one year. The shape of its orbit is not quite a perfect circle. It’s more like an oval, which causes Earth’s distance from the sun to vary during the year. Earth is nearest the sun, or at “perihelion,” in January when it’s about 91 million miles away. Earth is farthest from the sun, or at “aphelion,” in July when it’s about 95 million miles away.", "published_or_updated": "2026-11-18T13:24:25-05:00", "selector": "What Is Earth? > How Does Earth Move? > p[16]", "sha256_of_page": "2e3b5a2a0636dd0ce1284cf3817112c7268eb8e7a26259d26a696c1fa33f2644", "sha256_of_paragraph": "2e1e3774784c0643dd8cc4996a7aa144b3f9376491c1c8711436ff528049502a", "source_id": "NASA-EARTH", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/learning-resources/for-kids-and-students/what-is-earth/"}
{"char_offset": [3188, 3511], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:05Z", "heading_path": ["What Is Earth?", "How Does Earth Move?"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 17, "paragraph_text": "At the equator, Earth spins at just over 1,000 miles per hour. Earth makes a full spin around its axis once every 24 hours, or one day. The axis is an imaginary line through the center of the planet from the North Pole to the South Pole. Rather than straight up and down, Earth’s axis is tilted at an angle of 23.5 degrees.", "published_or_updated": "2026-11-18T13:24:25-05:00", "selector": "What Is Earth? > How Does Earth Move? > p[17]", "sha256_of_page": "2e3b5a2a0636dd0ce1284cf3817112c7268eb8e7a26259d26a696c1fa33f2644", "sha256_of_paragraph": "03bd64842a8a5022021f287eb362f288763e9b1d4a830e30a4bcf7bb8092e2b5", "source_id": "NASA-EARTH", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/learning-resources/for-kids-and-students/what-is-earth/"}
{"char_offset": [3512, 3876], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:05Z", "heading_path": ["What Is Earth?", "Why Do We Have Day and Night?"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 18, "paragraph_text": "At all times, half of Earth is lighted by the sun and half is in darkness. Areas facing toward the sun experience daytime. Areas facing away from the sun experience nighttime. As the planet spins, most places on Earth cycle through day and night once every 24 hours. The North Pole and South Pole have continuous daylight or darkness depending on the time of year.", "published_or_updated": "2026-11-18T13:24:25-05:00", "selector": "What Is Earth? > Why Do We Have Day and Night? > p[18]", "sha256_of_page": "2e3b5a2a0636dd0ce1284cf3817112c7268eb8e7a26259d26a696c1fa33f2644", "sha256_of_paragraph": "415d47395ce77a841f1add624a2ff277304f9aea14a2b3ee1b66b098d093aec6", "source_id": "NASA-EARTH", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/learning-resources/for-kids-and-students/what-is-earth/"}
{"char_offset": [3877, 4022], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:05Z", "heading_path": ["What Is Earth?", "Why Does Earth Have Seasons?"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 19, "paragraph_text": "Earth has seasons because its axis is tilted. Thus, the sun’s rays hit different parts of the planet more directly depending on the time of year.", "published_or_updated": "2026-11-18T13:24:25-05:00", "selector": "What Is Earth? > Why Does Earth Have Seasons? > p[19]", "sha256_of_page": "2e3b5a2a0636dd0ce1284cf3817112c7268eb8e7a26259d26a696c1fa33f2644", "sha256_of_paragraph": "164f46df95be105e6868bb36d89701fe693c31701e714dc76a5ffc1ee757ec63", "source_id": "NASA-EARTH", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/learning-resources/for-kids-and-students/what-is-earth/"}
{"char_offset": [4023, 4247], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:05Z", "heading_path": ["What Is Earth?", "Why Does Earth Have Seasons?"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 20, "paragraph_text": "From June to August, the sun’s rays hit the Northern Hemisphere more directly than the Southern Hemisphere. The result is warm (summer) weather in the Northern Hemisphere and cold (winter) weather in the Southern Hemisphere.", "published_or_updated": "2026-11-18T13:24:25-05:00", "selector": "What Is Earth? > Why Does Earth Have Seasons? > p[20]", "sha256_of_page": "2e3b5a2a0636dd0ce1284cf3817112c7268eb8e7a26259d26a696c1fa33f2644", "sha256_of_paragraph": "1d9de0a3d2569b3aadaefc927d288ad668d35881dd717fb79ca0e7fc58727c62", "source_id": "NASA-EARTH", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/learning-resources/for-kids-and-students/what-is-earth/"}
{"char_offset": [4248, 4478], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:05Z", "heading_path": ["What Is Earth?", "Why Does Earth Have Seasons?"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 21, "paragraph_text": "From December to February, the sun’s rays hit the Northern Hemisphere less directly than the Southern Hemisphere. The result is cold (winter) weather in the Northern Hemisphere and warm (summer) weather in the Southern Hemisphere.", "published_or_updated": "2026-11-18T13:24:25-05:00", "selector": "What Is Earth? > Why Does Earth Have Seasons? > p[21]", "sha256_of_page": "2e3b5a2a0636dd0ce1284cf3817112c7268eb8e7a26259d26a696c1fa33f2644", "sha256_of_paragraph": "3bad9f7fd671b0438acba345e466292adb6407a3f362a4b56e7ba94c3c683643", "source_id": "NASA-EARTH", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/learning-resources/for-kids-and-students/what-is-earth/"}
{"char_offset": [4479, 4635], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:05Z", "heading_path": ["What Is Earth?", "Why Does Earth Have Seasons?"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 22, "paragraph_text": "From September to November, the sun shines equally on both hemispheres. The result is fall in the Northern Hemisphere and spring in the Southern Hemisphere.", "published_or_updated": "2026-11-18T13:24:25-05:00", "selector": "What Is Earth? > Why Does Earth Have Seasons? > p[22]", "sha256_of_page": "2e3b5a2a0636dd0ce1284cf3817112c7268eb8e7a26259d26a696c1fa33f2644", "sha256_of_paragraph": "d1657d84e2252b23ba2e594306776c41d866b2550fec5780bbb9c41b0b7b87f6", "source_id": "NASA-EARTH", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/learning-resources/for-kids-and-students/what-is-earth/"}
{"char_offset": [4636, 4787], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:05Z", "heading_path": ["What Is Earth?", "Why Does Earth Have Seasons?"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 23, "paragraph_text": "The sun also shines equally on both hemispheres from March to May. The result is spring in the Northern Hemisphere and fall in the Southern Hemisphere.", "published_or_updated": "2026-11-18T13:24:25-05:00", "selector": "What Is Earth? > Why Does Earth Have Seasons? > p[23]", "sha256_of_page": "2e3b5a2a0636dd0ce1284cf3817112c7268eb8e7a26259d26a696c1fa33f2644", "sha256_of_paragraph": "8d9fc3e3d3009dd8f9e5acdaf60d5cc33901bda694f42691b03f24f56e4f3aa9", "source_id": "NASA-EARTH", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/learning-resources/for-kids-and-students/what-is-earth/"}
{"char_offset": [4788, 5175], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:05Z", "heading_path": ["What Is Earth?", "What Are Earth’s Different Parts?"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 24, "paragraph_text": "Earth consists of land, air, water and life. The land contains mountains, valleys and flat areas. The air is made up of different gases, mainly nitrogen and oxygen. The water includes oceans, lakes, rivers, streams, rain, snow and ice. Life consists of people, animals and plants. There are millions of species, or kinds of life, on Earth. Their sizes range from very tiny to very large.", "published_or_updated": "2026-11-18T13:24:25-05:00", "selector": "What Is Earth? > What Are Earth’s Different Parts? > p[24]", "sha256_of_page": "2e3b5a2a0636dd0ce1284cf3817112c7268eb8e7a26259d26a696c1fa33f2644", "sha256_of_paragraph": "62e2eea99cdb773343f52eb1db678575e1b26fded1c40f9639bfdefa7dc70f79", "source_id": "NASA-EARTH", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/learning-resources/for-kids-and-students/what-is-earth/"}
{"char_offset": [5176, 5331], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:05Z", "heading_path": ["What Is Earth?", "What Are Earth’s Different Parts?"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 25, "paragraph_text": "Below Earth’s surface are layers of rock and metal. Temperatures increase with depth, all the way to about 12,000 degrees Fahrenheit at Earth’s inner core.", "published_or_updated": "2026-11-18T13:24:25-05:00", "selector": "What Is Earth? > What Are Earth’s Different Parts? > p[25]", "sha256_of_page": "2e3b5a2a0636dd0ce1284cf3817112c7268eb8e7a26259d26a696c1fa33f2644", "sha256_of_paragraph": "5cac463665536e613c615c3d11f36fd88ab49ec6f3ef30a72057ad7c74fccc43", "source_id": "NASA-EARTH", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/learning-resources/for-kids-and-students/what-is-earth/"}
{"char_offset": [5332, 5525], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:05Z", "heading_path": ["What Is Earth?", "What Are Earth’s Different Parts?"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 26, "paragraph_text": "Earth’s parts once were seen as largely separate from each other. But now they are viewed together as the “Earth system.” Each part connects to and affects each of the other parts. For example:", "published_or_updated": "2026-11-18T13:24:25-05:00", "selector": "What Is Earth? > What Are Earth’s Different Parts? > p[26]", "sha256_of_page": "2e3b5a2a0636dd0ce1284cf3817112c7268eb8e7a26259d26a696c1fa33f2644", "sha256_of_paragraph": "6df35e060be5fdf4ff2882e3948a3bacdaea8eeadb40d9ff43a99cc76861777b", "source_id": "NASA-EARTH", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/learning-resources/for-kids-and-students/what-is-earth/"}
{"char_offset": [5526, 5571], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:05Z", "heading_path": ["What Is Earth?", "What Are Earth’s Different Parts?"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 27, "paragraph_text": "Clouds in the air drop rain and snow on land.", "published_or_updated": "2026-11-18T13:24:25-05:00", "selector": "What Is Earth? > What Are Earth’s Different Parts? > p[27]", "sha256_of_page": "2e3b5a2a0636dd0ce1284cf3817112c7268eb8e7a26259d26a696c1fa33f2644", "sha256_of_paragraph": "1b81f514b354115441e454c560d5978091958a00f0f248ac6b26aed80c965034", "source_id": "NASA-EARTH", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/learning-resources/for-kids-and-students/what-is-earth/"}
{"char_offset": [5572, 5611], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:05Z", "heading_path": ["What Is Earth?", "What Are Earth’s Different Parts?"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 28, "paragraph_text": "Water gives life to plants and animals.", "published_or_updated": "2026-11-18T13:24:25-05:00", "selector": "What Is Earth? > What Are Earth’s Different Parts? > p[28]", "sha256_of_page": "2e3b5a2a0636dd0ce1284cf3817112c7268eb8e7a26259d26a696c1fa33f2644", "sha256_of_paragraph": "d5dd2269d2a221e39e26d2e56b6db71e5147aef3c2addd33675bcf1c761b8e5c", "source_id": "NASA-EARTH", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/learning-resources/for-kids-and-students/what-is-earth/"}
{"char_offset": [5612, 5661], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:05Z", "heading_path": ["What Is Earth?", "What Are Earth’s Different Parts?"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 29, "paragraph_text": "Volcanoes on land send gas and dust into the air.", "published_or_updated": "2026-11-18T13:24:25-05:00", "selector": "What Is Earth? > What Are Earth’s Different Parts? > p[29]", "sha256_of_page": "2e3b5a2a0636dd0ce1284cf3817112c7268eb8e7a26259d26a696c1fa33f2644", "sha256_of_paragraph": "2a2f9b29a6a2613c91045c31d1023f0d17180ae46ec9b8e0c2611888f9433c03", "source_id": "NASA-EARTH", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/learning-resources/for-kids-and-students/what-is-earth/"}
{"char_offset": [5662, 5697], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:05Z", "heading_path": ["What Is Earth?", "What Are Earth’s Different Parts?"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 30, "paragraph_text": "People breathe air and drink water.", "published_or_updated": "2026-11-18T13:24:25-05:00", "selector": "What Is Earth? > What Are Earth’s Different Parts? > p[30]", "sha256_of_page": "2e3b5a2a0636dd0ce1284cf3817112c7268eb8e7a26259d26a696c1fa33f2644", "sha256_of_paragraph": "ff90053ee66fad8da428cfdddf5c9bf7e2dca4653cc63777681c9dee8f00555c", "source_id": "NASA-EARTH", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/learning-resources/for-kids-and-students/what-is-earth/"}
{"char_offset": [5698, 5790], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:05Z", "heading_path": ["What Is Earth?", "What Are Earth’s Different Parts?"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 31, "paragraph_text": "Earth system science is the study of interactions between and among Earth’s different parts.", "published_or_updated": "2026-11-18T13:24:25-05:00", "selector": "What Is Earth? > What Are Earth’s Different Parts? > p[31]", "sha256_of_page": "2e3b5a2a0636dd0ce1284cf3817112c7268eb8e7a26259d26a696c1fa33f2644", "sha256_of_paragraph": "9b7b9d6e4c7727e32b7e0516364605b63fe06bd4296d20e9473f7d8389437cef", "source_id": "NASA-EARTH", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/learning-resources/for-kids-and-students/what-is-earth/"}
{"char_offset": [5791, 6142], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:05Z", "heading_path": ["What Is Earth?", "Why and How Does NASA Study Earth?"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 32, "paragraph_text": "NASA studies Earth to learn about how the planet changes. Earth’s parts – land, air, water and life – are always changing. Some of the changes are natural and some are caused by humans. Scientists want to understand how Earth has changed in the past and how it is changing now. This information helps them predict how Earth might change in the future.", "published_or_updated": "2026-11-18T13:24:25-05:00", "selector": "What Is Earth? > Why and How Does NASA Study Earth? > p[32]", "sha256_of_page": "2e3b5a2a0636dd0ce1284cf3817112c7268eb8e7a26259d26a696c1fa33f2644", "sha256_of_paragraph": "1a6752acbcccd269431ed1aeb0258e822eff076f8b2cd6c6046e873b0b5eecaf", "source_id": "NASA-EARTH", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/learning-resources/for-kids-and-students/what-is-earth/"}
{"char_offset": [6143, 6575], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:05Z", "heading_path": ["What Is Earth?", "Why and How Does NASA Study Earth?"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 33, "paragraph_text": "NASA studies Earth using satellites. Satellites look toward Earth from space. They take pictures of, and collect information about, all of Earth’s parts. NASA satellites are especially good for observing clouds, oceans, land and ice. They also measure gases in the atmosphere, such as ozone and carbon dioxide. They measure how much energy enters and leaves Earth’s atmosphere. And they monitor wildfires, volcanoes and their smoke.", "published_or_updated": "2026-11-18T13:24:25-05:00", "selector": "What Is Earth? > Why and How Does NASA Study Earth? > p[33]", "sha256_of_page": "2e3b5a2a0636dd0ce1284cf3817112c7268eb8e7a26259d26a696c1fa33f2644", "sha256_of_paragraph": "715ad49512a0cdba0559836c15e95fd456a5ca49a4b98c9b0042f6d0879772a6", "source_id": "NASA-EARTH", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/learning-resources/for-kids-and-students/what-is-earth/"}
{"char_offset": [6576, 6855], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:05Z", "heading_path": ["What Is Earth?", "Why and How Does NASA Study Earth?"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 34, "paragraph_text": "Information gathered by NASA satellites helps scientists predict weather and climate. It also helps public health officials track disease and famine. It helps farmers decide when to plant crops and what kinds to plant. And it helps emergency workers respond to natural disasters.", "published_or_updated": "2026-11-18T13:24:25-05:00", "selector": "What Is Earth? > Why and How Does NASA Study Earth? > p[34]", "sha256_of_page": "2e3b5a2a0636dd0ce1284cf3817112c7268eb8e7a26259d26a696c1fa33f2644", "sha256_of_paragraph": "4712df47e5f803253af972ef0c7326b3c4b3bf129fdfca55e23a0e79a4d645f4", "source_id": "NASA-EARTH", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/learning-resources/for-kids-and-students/what-is-earth/"}
{"char_offset": [6856, 6963], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:05Z", "heading_path": ["What Is Earth?", "Why and How Does NASA Study Earth?"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 35, "paragraph_text": "The more people know about Earth and its current and predicted changes, the better decisions they can make.", "published_or_updated": "2026-11-18T13:24:25-05:00", "selector": "What Is Earth? > Why and How Does NASA Study Earth? > p[35]", "sha256_of_page": "2e3b5a2a0636dd0ce1284cf3817112c7268eb8e7a26259d26a696c1fa33f2644", "sha256_of_paragraph": "725b6defb99adfbeec7e8ca96ef17845c536798c6a473a1c3fa176c927a4f9c0", "source_id": "NASA-EARTH", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/learning-resources/for-kids-and-students/what-is-earth/"}
{"char_offset": [6964, 7015], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:05Z", "heading_path": ["What Is Earth?", "More About Earth"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 36, "paragraph_text": "Space Place in a Snap: The Solar System’s Formation", "published_or_updated": "2026-11-18T13:24:25-05:00", "selector": "What Is Earth? > More About Earth > p[36]", "sha256_of_page": "2e3b5a2a0636dd0ce1284cf3817112c7268eb8e7a26259d26a696c1fa33f2644", "sha256_of_paragraph": "3a9433bbcdcb866054596c52a7a8b975c79209689f1c8fd688f8afb530cde862", "source_id": "NASA-EARTH", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/learning-resources/for-kids-and-students/what-is-earth/"}
{"char_offset": [7016, 7048], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:05Z", "heading_path": ["What Is Earth?", "More About Earth"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 37, "paragraph_text": "Read What Is Earth? (Grades K-4)", "published_or_updated": "2026-11-18T13:24:25-05:00", "selector": "What Is Earth? > More About Earth > p[37]", "sha256_of_page": "2e3b5a2a0636dd0ce1284cf3817112c7268eb8e7a26259d26a696c1fa33f2644", "sha256_of_paragraph": "6e0650a1bab56a107b54250ede793a3eff1417e19e9d9d5bb9131f067bd21194", "source_id": "NASA-EARTH", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/learning-resources/for-kids-and-students/what-is-earth/"}
{"char_offset": [0, 65], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:10Z", "heading_path": [], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 0, "paragraph_text": "Why Are Planets Round? | NASA Space Place – NASA Science for Kids", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "p[0]", "sha256_of_page": "b83bc182f58bcdfb690f1c7e3bd1f8782e4a4d79a16fc26f73b042808e0641d0", "sha256_of_paragraph": "484320092adcbc59708501e7d95b9185f8f008d4c6d673964da5662aee6f2d3a", "source_id": "NASA-ROUND", "tag": "p", "url": "https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/planets-round/en/"}
{"char_offset": [66, 319], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:10Z", "heading_path": ["Why Are Planets Round?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 1, "paragraph_text": "A planet is round because of gravity. A planet's gravity pulls equally from all sides. Gravity pulls from the center to the edges like the spokes of a bicycle wheel. This makes the overall shape of a planet a sphere, which is a three-dimensional circle.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Why Are Planets Round? > p[1]", "sha256_of_page": "b83bc182f58bcdfb690f1c7e3bd1f8782e4a4d79a16fc26f73b042808e0641d0", "sha256_of_paragraph": "741d5212445f7b8cb238933ca700c3c462ec281032f250bf8708989f934a0ed0", "source_id": "NASA-ROUND", "tag": "p", "url": "https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/planets-round/en/"}
{"char_offset": [320, 606], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:10Z", "heading_path": ["Why Are Planets Round?", "Big, small, but all round"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 2, "paragraph_text": "The eight planets in our solar system differ in lots of ways. They are different sizes. They are different distances from the sun. Some are small and rocky, and others are big and gassy. But they're all nice and round. Why is that? Why aren't they shaped like cubes, pyramids, or discs?", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Why Are Planets Round? > Big, small, but all round > p[2]", "sha256_of_page": "b83bc182f58bcdfb690f1c7e3bd1f8782e4a4d79a16fc26f73b042808e0641d0", "sha256_of_paragraph": "a05a61f4c9635267944aa6559ff8ddc442e803b68351a8fbdf6e7f58f4e8163f", "source_id": "NASA-ROUND", "tag": "p", "url": "https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/planets-round/en/"}
{"char_offset": [607, 939], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:10Z", "heading_path": ["Why Are Planets Round?", "Big, small, but all round"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 3, "paragraph_text": "Planets form when material in space starts to bump and clump together. After a while it has enough stuff to have a good amount of gravity. That's the force that holds stuff together in space. When a forming planet is big enough, it starts to clear its path around the star it orbits. It uses its gravity to snag bits of space stuff.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Why Are Planets Round? > Big, small, but all round > p[3]", "sha256_of_page": "b83bc182f58bcdfb690f1c7e3bd1f8782e4a4d79a16fc26f73b042808e0641d0", "sha256_of_paragraph": "f7477258f46a0ef46fe442606bbad2139cc94f2a872ba5c3f8467c9b37f98658", "source_id": "NASA-ROUND", "tag": "p", "url": "https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/planets-round/en/"}
{"char_offset": [940, 1155], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:10Z", "heading_path": ["Why Are Planets Round?", "Big, small, but all round"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 4, "paragraph_text": "A planet's gravity pulls equally from all sides. Gravity pulls from the center to the edges like the spokes of a bicycle wheel. This makes the overall shape of a planet a sphere, which is a three-dimensional circle.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Why Are Planets Round? > Big, small, but all round > p[4]", "sha256_of_page": "b83bc182f58bcdfb690f1c7e3bd1f8782e4a4d79a16fc26f73b042808e0641d0", "sha256_of_paragraph": "e9dac8e1519c3fd9d1817b40009065b80566ffc29a42ea47678e1e4b0a230c07", "source_id": "NASA-ROUND", "tag": "p", "url": "https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/planets-round/en/"}
{"char_offset": [1156, 1337], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:10Z", "heading_path": ["Why Are Planets Round?", "Are they all perfect, though?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 5, "paragraph_text": "While all the planets in our solar system are nice and round, some are rounder than others. Mercury and Venus are the roundest of all. They are nearly perfect spheres, like marbles.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Why Are Planets Round? > Are they all perfect, though? > p[5]", "sha256_of_page": "b83bc182f58bcdfb690f1c7e3bd1f8782e4a4d79a16fc26f73b042808e0641d0", "sha256_of_paragraph": "ec296393d8069530b5113a6164e6f33e76fc07f2689d3c77ac07d9cbcf0a5f4a", "source_id": "NASA-ROUND", "tag": "p", "url": "https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/planets-round/en/"}
{"char_offset": [1338, 1387], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:10Z", "heading_path": ["Why Are Planets Round?", "Are they all perfect, though?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 6, "paragraph_text": "But some planets aren't quite so perfectly round.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Why Are Planets Round? > Are they all perfect, though? > p[6]", "sha256_of_page": "b83bc182f58bcdfb690f1c7e3bd1f8782e4a4d79a16fc26f73b042808e0641d0", "sha256_of_paragraph": "a50e4999694c13c44f214046642d005ba1bb7a12fb954fc50bafce72dd912632", "source_id": "NASA-ROUND", "tag": "p", "url": "https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/planets-round/en/"}
{"char_offset": [1388, 1780], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:10Z", "heading_path": ["Why Are Planets Round?", "Are they all perfect, though?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 7, "paragraph_text": "Saturn and Jupiter are bit thicker in the middle. As they spin around, they bulge out along the equator. Why does that happen? When something spins, like a planet as it rotates, things on the outer edge have to move faster than things on the inside to keep up. This is true for anything that spins, like a wheel, a DVD, or a fan. Things along the edge have to travel the farthest and fastest.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Why Are Planets Round? > Are they all perfect, though? > p[7]", "sha256_of_page": "b83bc182f58bcdfb690f1c7e3bd1f8782e4a4d79a16fc26f73b042808e0641d0", "sha256_of_paragraph": "e7aa623443f6fe440997b643e3e4bf8e4e7032a7b7e7e444af7473fb391440f9", "source_id": "NASA-ROUND", "tag": "p", "url": "https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/planets-round/en/"}
{"char_offset": [1781, 2157], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:10Z", "heading_path": ["Why Are Planets Round?", "Are they all perfect, though?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 8, "paragraph_text": "Along the equator of a planet, a circle half way between the north and south poles, gravity is holding the edges in but, as it spins, stuff wants to spin out like mud flying off a tire. Saturn and Jupiter are really big and spinning really fast but gravity still manages to hold them together. That's why they bulge in the middle. We call the extra width the equatorial bulge.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Why Are Planets Round? > Are they all perfect, though? > p[8]", "sha256_of_page": "b83bc182f58bcdfb690f1c7e3bd1f8782e4a4d79a16fc26f73b042808e0641d0", "sha256_of_paragraph": "402def308868cc85a11eb373b90904a3cee29e2a541df92c17e0c61507b44ef5", "source_id": "NASA-ROUND", "tag": "p", "url": "https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/planets-round/en/"}
{"char_offset": [2158, 2406], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:10Z", "heading_path": ["Why Are Planets Round?", "Are they all perfect, though?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 9, "paragraph_text": "Saturn bulges the most of all the planets in our solar system. If you compare the diameter from pole to pole to the diameter along the equator, it's not the same. Saturn is 10.7% thicker around the middle. Jupiter is 6.9% thicker around the middle.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Why Are Planets Round? > Are they all perfect, though? > p[9]", "sha256_of_page": "b83bc182f58bcdfb690f1c7e3bd1f8782e4a4d79a16fc26f73b042808e0641d0", "sha256_of_paragraph": "9f4b2bfcfa45ce906861b6a4c30268e5ade86e4f60718211c3e8a0f89e47a1da", "source_id": "NASA-ROUND", "tag": "p", "url": "https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/planets-round/en/"}
{"char_offset": [2407, 2521], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:10Z", "heading_path": ["Why Are Planets Round?", "Are they all perfect, though?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 10, "paragraph_text": "Instead of being perfectly round like marbles, they are like basketballs squished down while someone sits on them.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Why Are Planets Round? > Are they all perfect, though? > p[10]", "sha256_of_page": "b83bc182f58bcdfb690f1c7e3bd1f8782e4a4d79a16fc26f73b042808e0641d0", "sha256_of_paragraph": "b95ca977deeee33c77ba8743c0b96f29e95f6ee49775ee5c4b6458d0a83db7b3", "source_id": "NASA-ROUND", "tag": "p", "url": "https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/planets-round/en/"}
{"char_offset": [2522, 2857], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:10Z", "heading_path": ["Why Are Planets Round?", "What about the other planets?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 11, "paragraph_text": "Earth and Mars are small and don't spin around as fast as the gas giants. They aren't perfect spheres, but they are rounder than Saturn and Jupiter. Earth is 0.3% thicker in the middle, and Mars is 0.6% thicker in the middle. Since they're not even one whole percentage point thicker in the middle, it's safe to say they're very round.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Why Are Planets Round? > What about the other planets? > p[11]", "sha256_of_page": "b83bc182f58bcdfb690f1c7e3bd1f8782e4a4d79a16fc26f73b042808e0641d0", "sha256_of_paragraph": "75231d49597034aac00832f7e4fa8740400dd89f6104d1d6b80545b13ecc003a", "source_id": "NASA-ROUND", "tag": "p", "url": "https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/planets-round/en/"}
{"char_offset": [2858, 3022], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:10Z", "heading_path": ["Why Are Planets Round?", "What about the other planets?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 12, "paragraph_text": "As for Uranus and Neptune, they're in between. Uranus is 2.3% thicker in the middle. Neptune is 1.7% thicker. They're not perfectly round, but they're pretty close.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Why Are Planets Round? > What about the other planets? > p[12]", "sha256_of_page": "b83bc182f58bcdfb690f1c7e3bd1f8782e4a4d79a16fc26f73b042808e0641d0", "sha256_of_paragraph": "97409fed22b46b15502e9a356a51448c237a908c208026b29e243b666f61f2e0", "source_id": "NASA-ROUND", "tag": "p", "url": "https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/planets-round/en/"}
{"char_offset": [3023, 3521], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:10Z", "heading_path": ["Why Are Planets Round?", "What about the other planets?", "Feel the forces from spinning"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 13, "paragraph_text": "Do you want to know what it's like to be a spinning planet? You can feel it when you spin around in place. First, make sure there are no obstacles around that you might bump into. Then either while standing, or in a spinner chair, spin around in circles. Hold your arms close to your body, then extend your arms out. Move your arms in and out and feel the difference. When your arms are outstretched, your hands have to move faster than your shoulders to keep up, so you'll feel more force on them.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Why Are Planets Round? > What about the other planets? > Feel the forces from spinning > p[13]", "sha256_of_page": "b83bc182f58bcdfb690f1c7e3bd1f8782e4a4d79a16fc26f73b042808e0641d0", "sha256_of_paragraph": "d3cb017ec433a287dad5fd2d087d47c5eb1f3eb0f62aa2b2d98fafee27c98f5f", "source_id": "NASA-ROUND", "tag": "p", "url": "https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/planets-round/en/"}
{"char_offset": [3522, 3556], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:10Z", "heading_path": ["Why Are Planets Round?", "What about the other planets?", "Feel the forces from spinning"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 14, "paragraph_text": "article last updated June 27, 2019", "published_or_updated": "June 27, 2019", "selector": "Why Are Planets Round? > What about the other planets? > Feel the forces from spinning > p[14]", "sha256_of_page": "b83bc182f58bcdfb690f1c7e3bd1f8782e4a4d79a16fc26f73b042808e0641d0", "sha256_of_paragraph": "74d337db30b89edc643d4dff9312f0c97cf63b2b6f8196542a4529ca32d13f63", "source_id": "NASA-ROUND", "tag": "p", "url": "https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/planets-round/en/"}
