{"char_offset": [2841, 2937], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:53:49Z", "heading_path": ["Call Our Hotline"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "archived_copy", "paragraph_index": 62, "paragraph_text": "For help with meat, poultry, and egg products, call the toll-free USDA Meat and Poultry Hotline:", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Call Our Hotline > p[62]", "sha256_of_page": "a3010e76a59170a0f610de828fd54011639e828730bad77e6f455bb1c995d19f", "sha256_of_paragraph": "0ba850b3d584c90c8ca732836468e8072b6b798cd75b3ba76cd8e6eda4a2a932", "source_id": "USDA-MW", "tag": "p", "url": "http://web.archive.org/web/20261119141358/https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/cooking-microwave-ovens"}
{"char_offset": [2938, 2968], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:53:49Z", "heading_path": ["Call Our Hotline"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "archived_copy", "paragraph_index": 63, "paragraph_text": "1-888-MPHOTLINE 1-888-674-6854", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Call Our Hotline > p[63]", "sha256_of_page": "a3010e76a59170a0f610de828fd54011639e828730bad77e6f455bb1c995d19f", "sha256_of_paragraph": "8648a9e8f81cef16857888717790e208ed58828abac83a21c4ab52398dcf3c87", "source_id": "USDA-MW", "tag": "p", "url": "http://web.archive.org/web/20261119141358/https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/cooking-microwave-ovens"}
{"char_offset": [2969, 3077], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:53:49Z", "heading_path": ["Call Our Hotline", "Report a Problem with Food Online"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "archived_copy", "paragraph_index": 64, "paragraph_text": "If you have a problem with a food product, let FSIS know or find the appropriate public health organization.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Call Our Hotline > Report a Problem with Food Online > p[64]", "sha256_of_page": "a3010e76a59170a0f610de828fd54011639e828730bad77e6f455bb1c995d19f", "sha256_of_paragraph": "f381bddb298d53d13ba8b9db55efcfc7d1e4d8a63da65c81e3f2784eb5bf849d", "source_id": "USDA-MW", "tag": "p", "url": "http://web.archive.org/web/20261119141358/https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/cooking-microwave-ovens"}
{"char_offset": [3078, 3697], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:53:49Z", "heading_path": ["Cooking with Microwave Ovens"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "archived_copy", "paragraph_index": 65, "paragraph_text": "The microwave oven is one of the great inventions of the 20th century; over 90% of homes in America have at least one. Microwave ovens can play an important role at mealtime, but special care must be taken when cooking or reheating meat, poultry, fish, and eggs to make sure they are prepared safely. Microwave ovens can cook unevenly and leave \"cold spots\" where harmful bacteria can survive. For this reason, it is important to use a food thermometer and test food in several places to be sure it has reached the recommended safe temperature to destroy bacteria and other pathogens that could cause foodborne illness.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Cooking with Microwave Ovens > p[65]", "sha256_of_page": "a3010e76a59170a0f610de828fd54011639e828730bad77e6f455bb1c995d19f", "sha256_of_paragraph": "46b3f5fdb04ba8c14b7da4c26587b111d61968e2b6e2efe5e33f71f0790b5325", "source_id": "USDA-MW", "tag": "p", "url": "http://web.archive.org/web/20261119141358/https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/cooking-microwave-ovens"}
{"char_offset": [3698, 4078], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:53:49Z", "heading_path": ["Cooking with Microwave Ovens", "How do microwaves cook food?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "archived_copy", "paragraph_index": 66, "paragraph_text": "The magnetron inside the oven converts ordinary electric power from a wall socket into very short radio waves (around 4 inches from crest to crest). They are transmitted by the oven's magnetron at a frequency of about 2450 Megahertz. At that frequency, power is readily absorbed by water, fats and sugars, resulting in very fast vibration and high temperatures that cook the food.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Cooking with Microwave Ovens > How do microwaves cook food? > p[66]", "sha256_of_page": "a3010e76a59170a0f610de828fd54011639e828730bad77e6f455bb1c995d19f", "sha256_of_paragraph": "7fc4346d3e0c46e7fe0300b0580f8bf9ade6025b820666d087b4cbdc835cc66b", "source_id": "USDA-MW", "tag": "p", "url": "http://web.archive.org/web/20261119141358/https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/cooking-microwave-ovens"}
{"char_offset": [4079, 4414], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:53:49Z", "heading_path": ["Cooking with Microwave Ovens", "What are power levels?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "archived_copy", "paragraph_index": 67, "paragraph_text": "On high power, the food is subjected to the highest amount of microwave energy because the magnetron produces microwaves at full capacity. To produce a power level less than high (100%), the magnetron cycles off and on. For example, medium power (50%) means that the oven produces microwaves 50% of the time and is off 50% of the time.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Cooking with Microwave Ovens > What are power levels? > p[67]", "sha256_of_page": "a3010e76a59170a0f610de828fd54011639e828730bad77e6f455bb1c995d19f", "sha256_of_paragraph": "9ea0a39357920cae1a7943b5bcbb7d385c9868d70913fe4fe046aabffc341ece", "source_id": "USDA-MW", "tag": "p", "url": "http://web.archive.org/web/20261119141358/https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/cooking-microwave-ovens"}
{"char_offset": [4415, 4818], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:53:49Z", "heading_path": ["Cooking with Microwave Ovens", "What are power levels?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "archived_copy", "paragraph_index": 68, "paragraph_text": "Foods best to cook on high power are basically tender foods and those with a high moisture content such as ground beef, poultry, vegetables and fruits. Eggs, cheese and solid meat can toughen when microwaved on high. They are best cooked on reduced power. Large cuts of meat should be cooked on medium power (50%) for longer periods. This allows heat to reach the center without overcooking outer areas.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Cooking with Microwave Ovens > What are power levels? > p[68]", "sha256_of_page": "a3010e76a59170a0f610de828fd54011639e828730bad77e6f455bb1c995d19f", "sha256_of_paragraph": "725abc103ea67022bb69a3c7463172e82edcd8d6484edbe951b3b5db42add540", "source_id": "USDA-MW", "tag": "p", "url": "http://web.archive.org/web/20261119141358/https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/cooking-microwave-ovens"}
{"char_offset": [4819, 5041], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:53:49Z", "heading_path": ["Cooking with Microwave Ovens", "Do microwaves cook food from the inside out?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "archived_copy", "paragraph_index": 69, "paragraph_text": "No. Microwaves penetrate the food to a depth of 1 to 1½ inches. In thicker pieces of food, the microwaves don't reach the center. That area would cook by conduction of heat from the outer areas of the food into the middle.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Cooking with Microwave Ovens > Do microwaves cook food from the inside out? > p[69]", "sha256_of_page": "a3010e76a59170a0f610de828fd54011639e828730bad77e6f455bb1c995d19f", "sha256_of_paragraph": "5814562136be6fba146acfc66e34ba40089c2abff08c43948410b2f79920f7d4", "source_id": "USDA-MW", "tag": "p", "url": "http://web.archive.org/web/20261119141358/https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/cooking-microwave-ovens"}
{"char_offset": [5042, 5309], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:53:49Z", "heading_path": ["Cooking with Microwave Ovens", "Do microwaves cook food from the inside out?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "archived_copy", "paragraph_index": 70, "paragraph_text": "In a microwave oven, the air in the oven is at room temperature so the temperature of the food surface is cooler than food in a conventional oven where the food is heated by hot air. Therefore, food cooked in a microwave oven doesn't normally become brown and crispy.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Cooking with Microwave Ovens > Do microwaves cook food from the inside out? > p[70]", "sha256_of_page": "a3010e76a59170a0f610de828fd54011639e828730bad77e6f455bb1c995d19f", "sha256_of_paragraph": "134c19f87ed7401a1330412ca1b32ac0ebda5b406fa920a292394bac62204605", "source_id": "USDA-MW", "tag": "p", "url": "http://web.archive.org/web/20261119141358/https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/cooking-microwave-ovens"}
{"char_offset": [5310, 5571], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:53:49Z", "heading_path": ["Cooking with Microwave Ovens", "Do microwaves cook food safely?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "archived_copy", "paragraph_index": 71, "paragraph_text": "Bacteria will be destroyed during microwave cooking just as in other types of ovens, so food is safe cooked in a microwave oven. However the food can cook less evenly than in a conventional oven. Microwave cooking can be uneven just as with frying and grilling.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Cooking with Microwave Ovens > Do microwaves cook food safely? > p[71]", "sha256_of_page": "a3010e76a59170a0f610de828fd54011639e828730bad77e6f455bb1c995d19f", "sha256_of_paragraph": "8ce8a44d736a00c3c2ad5a0e6b049259c431aa3493d844279a7bb921ea603d19", "source_id": "USDA-MW", "tag": "p", "url": "http://web.archive.org/web/20261119141358/https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/cooking-microwave-ovens"}
{"char_offset": [5572, 5792], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:53:49Z", "heading_path": ["Cooking with Microwave Ovens", "Do microwaves cook food safely?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "archived_copy", "paragraph_index": 72, "paragraph_text": "For that reason, it is important to use a food thermometer and test food in several places to be sure it has reached the recommended temperature to destroy bacteria and other pathogens that could cause foodborne illness.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Cooking with Microwave Ovens > Do microwaves cook food safely? > p[72]", "sha256_of_page": "a3010e76a59170a0f610de828fd54011639e828730bad77e6f455bb1c995d19f", "sha256_of_paragraph": "8a1e6b99ab55ed1755a59385a52cea24fdb15394224315f2df761fab7cdcc29f", "source_id": "USDA-MW", "tag": "p", "url": "http://web.archive.org/web/20261119141358/https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/cooking-microwave-ovens"}
{"char_offset": [5793, 5984], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:53:49Z", "heading_path": ["Cooking with Microwave Ovens", "Do microwaves cook food safely?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "archived_copy", "paragraph_index": 73, "paragraph_text": "To promote uniform cooking, arrange food items evenly in a covered dish and add some liquid if needed. Where possible, debone large pieces of meat; bone can shield meat from thorough cooking.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Cooking with Microwave Ovens > Do microwaves cook food safely? > p[73]", "sha256_of_page": "a3010e76a59170a0f610de828fd54011639e828730bad77e6f455bb1c995d19f", "sha256_of_paragraph": "77237d58fe5e3a184d0ad819b3d22dfde70f166a69574a9d6857af47073d201a", "source_id": "USDA-MW", "tag": "p", "url": "http://web.archive.org/web/20261119141358/https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/cooking-microwave-ovens"}
{"char_offset": [5985, 6328], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:53:49Z", "heading_path": ["Cooking with Microwave Ovens", "Do microwaves cook food safely?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "archived_copy", "paragraph_index": 74, "paragraph_text": "Cover the dish with a lid or plastic wrap. Allow enough space between the food and the top of the dish so that plastic wrap does not touch the food. Loosen or vent the lid or wrap to allow steam to vent. The moist heat that is created will help destroy harmful bacteria and ensure uniform cooking. Cooking bags also provide safe, even cooking.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Cooking with Microwave Ovens > Do microwaves cook food safely? > p[74]", "sha256_of_page": "a3010e76a59170a0f610de828fd54011639e828730bad77e6f455bb1c995d19f", "sha256_of_paragraph": "3362b9397920d3abcd16a0f53108cbb29d83dd05151150c533e1e32db3c074ef", "source_id": "USDA-MW", "tag": "p", "url": "http://web.archive.org/web/20261119141358/https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/cooking-microwave-ovens"}
{"char_offset": [6329, 6602], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:53:49Z", "heading_path": ["Cooking with Microwave Ovens", "Do microwaves cook food safely?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "archived_copy", "paragraph_index": 75, "paragraph_text": "Stir, rotate, or turn foods upside down (where possible) midway through the microwaving time to even the cooking and eliminate cold spots where harmful bacteria can survive. Even if the microwave oven has a turntable, it's still helpful to stir and turn food top to bottom.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Cooking with Microwave Ovens > Do microwaves cook food safely? > p[75]", "sha256_of_page": "a3010e76a59170a0f610de828fd54011639e828730bad77e6f455bb1c995d19f", "sha256_of_paragraph": "3786feb30a0e10de286f8397362b8f24dd7d16388f261dd86754aed4df563bcb", "source_id": "USDA-MW", "tag": "p", "url": "http://web.archive.org/web/20261119141358/https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/cooking-microwave-ovens"}
{"char_offset": [6603, 6813], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:53:49Z", "heading_path": ["Cooking with Microwave Ovens", "Do microwaves cook food safely?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "archived_copy", "paragraph_index": 76, "paragraph_text": "Follow cooking instructions on product label (or recipe instructions). If a range of time is given, start with the fewest minutes recommended. Add cooking time if necessary to reach a safe internal temperature.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Cooking with Microwave Ovens > Do microwaves cook food safely? > p[76]", "sha256_of_page": "a3010e76a59170a0f610de828fd54011639e828730bad77e6f455bb1c995d19f", "sha256_of_paragraph": "cc14d84829371dfe7db74c74656d983d2200bc14c249cc291fa6bf98c8eac642", "source_id": "USDA-MW", "tag": "p", "url": "http://web.archive.org/web/20261119141358/https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/cooking-microwave-ovens"}
{"char_offset": [6814, 6957], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:53:49Z", "heading_path": ["Cooking with Microwave Ovens", "Do microwaves cook food safely?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "archived_copy", "paragraph_index": 77, "paragraph_text": "Observe the \"standing time.\" Cooking continues and is completed during standing time. Most importantly, follow the manufacturer's instructions.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Cooking with Microwave Ovens > Do microwaves cook food safely? > p[77]", "sha256_of_page": "a3010e76a59170a0f610de828fd54011639e828730bad77e6f455bb1c995d19f", "sha256_of_paragraph": "26aae377285942c068cb748081a70139b0bbf5ae149e0adee7ed27fe3d31ceca", "source_id": "USDA-MW", "tag": "p", "url": "http://web.archive.org/web/20261119141358/https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/cooking-microwave-ovens"}
{"char_offset": [6958, 7667], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:53:49Z", "heading_path": ["Cooking with Microwave Ovens", "What is \"standing time\"?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "archived_copy", "paragraph_index": 78, "paragraph_text": "Microwaves cause water, fat, and sugar molecules to vibrate 2.5 million times per second, producing heat. After the oven is off or food is removed from the oven, the molecules continue to generate heat as they come to a standstill. This additional cooking after microwaving stops is called \"carryover cooking time,\" \"resting time,\" or \"standing time.\" It occurs for a longer time in dense foods such as a whole turkey or beef roast than in less-dense foods like breads, small vegetables and fruits. During this time, the temperature of a food can increase several degrees. For that reason, directions may advise to let a food \"rest\" for a few minutes after turning off the oven or removing food from the oven.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Cooking with Microwave Ovens > What is \"standing time\"? > p[78]", "sha256_of_page": "a3010e76a59170a0f610de828fd54011639e828730bad77e6f455bb1c995d19f", "sha256_of_paragraph": "2a758ab102bd9834b5a3bc7a0b275395037e96f929319e3a845623a3480050b7", "source_id": "USDA-MW", "tag": "p", "url": "http://web.archive.org/web/20261119141358/https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/cooking-microwave-ovens"}
{"char_offset": [7668, 8035], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:53:49Z", "heading_path": ["Cooking with Microwave Ovens", "What is \"standing time\"?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "archived_copy", "paragraph_index": 79, "paragraph_text": "Use a food thermometer or the oven's temperature probe to verify the food has reached a safe internal temperature. Unless the food thermometer is labeled safe for microwave cooking, do not leave the thermometer in the food during microwaving. Instead, use an instant read food thermometer to test the temperature of the food after removing it from the microwave oven.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Cooking with Microwave Ovens > What is \"standing time\"? > p[79]", "sha256_of_page": "a3010e76a59170a0f610de828fd54011639e828730bad77e6f455bb1c995d19f", "sha256_of_paragraph": "1b99065b5d532e5cb30d1ef27dcf5d1f9c89647cd9f6702e88ec25a79f3bf358", "source_id": "USDA-MW", "tag": "p", "url": "http://web.archive.org/web/20261119141358/https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/cooking-microwave-ovens"}
{"char_offset": [8036, 8320], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:53:49Z", "heading_path": ["Cooking with Microwave Ovens", "What are the USDA recommended temperatures for microwaving food safely?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "archived_copy", "paragraph_index": 80, "paragraph_text": "Place the thermometer in the thickest area of the meat or poultry — not near fat or bone — and check the temperature in the innermost part of the thigh and wing and in the thickest part of the breast of whole poultry. Cooking times may vary because ovens vary in power and efficiency.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Cooking with Microwave Ovens > What are the USDA recommended temperatures for microwaving food safely? > p[80]", "sha256_of_page": "a3010e76a59170a0f610de828fd54011639e828730bad77e6f455bb1c995d19f", "sha256_of_paragraph": "124061d714a19ea6dcc75f874c706ec7c00f58062ee2514410a07746ddc2c957", "source_id": "USDA-MW", "tag": "p", "url": "http://web.archive.org/web/20261119141358/https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/cooking-microwave-ovens"}
{"char_offset": [8321, 8385], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:53:49Z", "heading_path": ["Cooking with Microwave Ovens", "What are the USDA recommended temperatures for microwaving food safely?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "archived_copy", "paragraph_index": 81, "paragraph_text": "Cook ground meats to 160 °F as measured with a food thermometer.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Cooking with Microwave Ovens > What are the USDA recommended temperatures for microwaving food safely? > p[81]", "sha256_of_page": "a3010e76a59170a0f610de828fd54011639e828730bad77e6f455bb1c995d19f", "sha256_of_paragraph": "9ec931b6d981f295ddaa5a66e86d6414161706210c275d1a3f9bbbc6a092ccd7", "source_id": "USDA-MW", "tag": "p", "url": "http://web.archive.org/web/20261119141358/https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/cooking-microwave-ovens"}
{"char_offset": [8386, 8766], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:53:49Z", "heading_path": ["Cooking with Microwave Ovens", "What are the USDA recommended temperatures for microwaving food safely?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "archived_copy", "paragraph_index": 82, "paragraph_text": "Cook all raw beef, pork, lamb and veal steaks, chops, and roasts to a minimum internal temperature of 145 °F as measured with a food thermometer before removing meat from the heat source. For safety and quality, allow meat to rest for at least three minutes before carving or consuming. For reasons of personal preference, consumers may choose to cook meat to higher temperatures.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Cooking with Microwave Ovens > What are the USDA recommended temperatures for microwaving food safely? > p[82]", "sha256_of_page": "a3010e76a59170a0f610de828fd54011639e828730bad77e6f455bb1c995d19f", "sha256_of_paragraph": "03151cad6c16b2a7a4d3ab6027ef1f207c6fdf6a7be6338bd4cd713369bb3c2b", "source_id": "USDA-MW", "tag": "p", "url": "http://web.archive.org/web/20261119141358/https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/cooking-microwave-ovens"}
{"char_offset": [8767, 8869], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:53:49Z", "heading_path": ["Cooking with Microwave Ovens", "What are the USDA recommended temperatures for microwaving food safely?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "archived_copy", "paragraph_index": 83, "paragraph_text": "Cook all poultry to a safe minimum internal temperature of 165 °F as measured with a food thermometer.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Cooking with Microwave Ovens > What are the USDA recommended temperatures for microwaving food safely? > p[83]", "sha256_of_page": "a3010e76a59170a0f610de828fd54011639e828730bad77e6f455bb1c995d19f", "sha256_of_paragraph": "acc12cbb270deacb6df0ec7998bd189ca0a40741701a4f166e958ccea9b44d34", "source_id": "USDA-MW", "tag": "p", "url": "http://web.archive.org/web/20261119141358/https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/cooking-microwave-ovens"}
{"char_offset": [8870, 8914], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:53:49Z", "heading_path": ["Cooking with Microwave Ovens", "What are the USDA recommended temperatures for microwaving food safely?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "archived_copy", "paragraph_index": 84, "paragraph_text": "Eggs and casseroles containing eggs, 160 °F.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Cooking with Microwave Ovens > What are the USDA recommended temperatures for microwaving food safely? > p[84]", "sha256_of_page": "a3010e76a59170a0f610de828fd54011639e828730bad77e6f455bb1c995d19f", "sha256_of_paragraph": "457468b44bd0285db70b1b1942363c68e5a8011dee43202a8c869e07c3629062", "source_id": "USDA-MW", "tag": "p", "url": "http://web.archive.org/web/20261119141358/https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/cooking-microwave-ovens"}
{"char_offset": [8915, 9101], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:53:49Z", "heading_path": ["Cooking with Microwave Ovens", "What are the USDA recommended temperatures for microwaving food safely?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "archived_copy", "paragraph_index": 85, "paragraph_text": "After removing food from the microwave, always allow standing time of at least 3 minutes. This completes the cooking process. Then check the internal temperature with a food thermometer.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Cooking with Microwave Ovens > What are the USDA recommended temperatures for microwaving food safely? > p[85]", "sha256_of_page": "a3010e76a59170a0f610de828fd54011639e828730bad77e6f455bb1c995d19f", "sha256_of_paragraph": "d6e9bdd5e6755e7f47bcfc59a2a413063cdf172c60df0d80148e717ae880902d", "source_id": "USDA-MW", "tag": "p", "url": "http://web.archive.org/web/20261119141358/https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/cooking-microwave-ovens"}
{"char_offset": [9102, 9407], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:53:49Z", "heading_path": ["Cooking with Microwave Ovens", "Is it safe to microwave food only until partially done?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "archived_copy", "paragraph_index": 86, "paragraph_text": "Never partially cook food and store it for later use because any bacteria present wouldn't have been destroyed. When partially cooking food in the microwave oven to finish cooking on the grill or in a conventional oven, it is important to transfer the microwaved food to the other heat source immediately.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Cooking with Microwave Ovens > Is it safe to microwave food only until partially done? > p[86]", "sha256_of_page": "a3010e76a59170a0f610de828fd54011639e828730bad77e6f455bb1c995d19f", "sha256_of_paragraph": "fe18f6cfce9e57d4cc9d887ce7b3c38462a634a3dd9ee70500a3d432c93c8c66", "source_id": "USDA-MW", "tag": "p", "url": "http://web.archive.org/web/20261119141358/https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/cooking-microwave-ovens"}
{"char_offset": [9408, 9630], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:53:49Z", "heading_path": ["Cooking with Microwave Ovens", "Is it safe to cook stuffed poultry in a microwave oven?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "archived_copy", "paragraph_index": 87, "paragraph_text": "Cooking whole, stuffed poultry in a microwave oven is not recommended. Because food cooks so quickly in a microwave oven, the stuffing might not have enough time to reach the temperature needed to destroy harmful bacteria.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Cooking with Microwave Ovens > Is it safe to cook stuffed poultry in a microwave oven? > p[87]", "sha256_of_page": "a3010e76a59170a0f610de828fd54011639e828730bad77e6f455bb1c995d19f", "sha256_of_paragraph": "12660f29a6d2667d097709188b56ffe93c90c84d9e5d50aa4c9b8530cbfea5ea", "source_id": "USDA-MW", "tag": "p", "url": "http://web.archive.org/web/20261119141358/https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/cooking-microwave-ovens"}
{"char_offset": [9631, 9868], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:53:49Z", "heading_path": ["Cooking with Microwave Ovens", "What is the best way to thaw frozen food in a microwave oven?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "archived_copy", "paragraph_index": 88, "paragraph_text": "Remove food from its packaging before defrosting. Do not use foam trays and plastic wraps because they are not heat stable at high temperatures. Melting or warping from the heat of the food may cause harmful chemicals to migrate into it.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Cooking with Microwave Ovens > What is the best way to thaw frozen food in a microwave oven? > p[88]", "sha256_of_page": "a3010e76a59170a0f610de828fd54011639e828730bad77e6f455bb1c995d19f", "sha256_of_paragraph": "f71de43b3c98a954a7b34b1093869492f087fb8ab39f8a818529e4bfedcb7450", "source_id": "USDA-MW", "tag": "p", "url": "http://web.archive.org/web/20261119141358/https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/cooking-microwave-ovens"}
{"char_offset": [9869, 10277], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:53:49Z", "heading_path": ["Cooking with Microwave Ovens", "What is the best way to thaw frozen food in a microwave oven?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "archived_copy", "paragraph_index": 89, "paragraph_text": "Select the \"defrost\" setting or 30% power. During microwave defrosting, rotate and turn food upside down where possible. For individual pieces such as chicken parts, break them apart when possible, rotate and turn upside down several times during defrosting. When thawing ground meats, scrape the thawed portion off as it softens; remove it from the oven. Continue to microwave defrost the remaining portion.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Cooking with Microwave Ovens > What is the best way to thaw frozen food in a microwave oven? > p[89]", "sha256_of_page": "a3010e76a59170a0f610de828fd54011639e828730bad77e6f455bb1c995d19f", "sha256_of_paragraph": "3b234b60335f3279a729a06e5809a18cd9139e823d9f44213bfa52069fc02fe9", "source_id": "USDA-MW", "tag": "p", "url": "http://web.archive.org/web/20261119141358/https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/cooking-microwave-ovens"}
{"char_offset": [10278, 10505], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:53:49Z", "heading_path": ["Cooking with Microwave Ovens", "What is the best way to thaw frozen food in a microwave oven?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "archived_copy", "paragraph_index": 90, "paragraph_text": "Cook meat, poultry, egg casseroles, and fish immediately after defrosting in the microwave oven because some areas of the frozen food may begin to cook during the defrosting time. Do not hold partially cooked food to use later.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Cooking with Microwave Ovens > What is the best way to thaw frozen food in a microwave oven? > p[90]", "sha256_of_page": "a3010e76a59170a0f610de828fd54011639e828730bad77e6f455bb1c995d19f", "sha256_of_paragraph": "ea19dac31722c6036a9f9ed3e16eafae696d58ba68f372de1aa826ad8bae5d2f", "source_id": "USDA-MW", "tag": "p", "url": "http://web.archive.org/web/20261119141358/https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/cooking-microwave-ovens"}
{"char_offset": [10506, 10655], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:53:49Z", "heading_path": ["Cooking with Microwave Ovens", "How do you safely reheat cooked food in a microwave oven?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "archived_copy", "paragraph_index": 91, "paragraph_text": "Cover foods with a lid or a microwave-safe plastic wrap to hold in moisture and provide safe, even heating. Turn back a corner for the steam to vent.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Cooking with Microwave Ovens > How do you safely reheat cooked food in a microwave oven? > p[91]", "sha256_of_page": "a3010e76a59170a0f610de828fd54011639e828730bad77e6f455bb1c995d19f", "sha256_of_paragraph": "da53637cadba1572f1f68abbe4e7912a2f16f11121433ff2f620d660beca52f8", "source_id": "USDA-MW", "tag": "p", "url": "http://web.archive.org/web/20261119141358/https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/cooking-microwave-ovens"}
{"char_offset": [10656, 10765], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:53:49Z", "heading_path": ["Cooking with Microwave Ovens", "How do you safely reheat cooked food in a microwave oven?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "archived_copy", "paragraph_index": 92, "paragraph_text": "Heat ready-to-eat foods such as hot dogs, luncheon meats, fully cooked ham, and leftovers until steaming hot.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Cooking with Microwave Ovens > How do you safely reheat cooked food in a microwave oven? > p[92]", "sha256_of_page": "a3010e76a59170a0f610de828fd54011639e828730bad77e6f455bb1c995d19f", "sha256_of_paragraph": "4e4ea822b602fd0895736f29fcc71c83e95938008068e3ce9131cbb241922792", "source_id": "USDA-MW", "tag": "p", "url": "http://web.archive.org/web/20261119141358/https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/cooking-microwave-ovens"}
{"char_offset": [10766, 10905], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:53:49Z", "heading_path": ["Cooking with Microwave Ovens", "How do you safely reheat cooked food in a microwave oven?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "archived_copy", "paragraph_index": 93, "paragraph_text": "After reheating foods in the microwave oven, allow standing time. Then, use a clean food thermometer to check that food has reached 165 °F.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Cooking with Microwave Ovens > How do you safely reheat cooked food in a microwave oven? > p[93]", "sha256_of_page": "a3010e76a59170a0f610de828fd54011639e828730bad77e6f455bb1c995d19f", "sha256_of_paragraph": "72c7cb0e9619e7016ac72387a8e74fc0e55815bd468112f8807b0d8143212b6d", "source_id": "USDA-MW", "tag": "p", "url": "http://web.archive.org/web/20261119141358/https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/cooking-microwave-ovens"}
{"char_offset": [10906, 11098], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:53:49Z", "heading_path": ["Cooking with Microwave Ovens", "What containers and wraps are safe to use in the microwave oven?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "archived_copy", "paragraph_index": 94, "paragraph_text": "Only use cookware that is specially manufactured for use in the microwave oven. Glass, ceramic containers, and all plastics that are safe to use usually will be labeled for microwave oven use.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Cooking with Microwave Ovens > What containers and wraps are safe to use in the microwave oven? > p[94]", "sha256_of_page": "a3010e76a59170a0f610de828fd54011639e828730bad77e6f455bb1c995d19f", "sha256_of_paragraph": "98488d6b88594c5b2787468a14e85e7252ef877025a069fe6a09b3cc19625253", "source_id": "USDA-MW", "tag": "p", "url": "http://web.archive.org/web/20261119141358/https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/cooking-microwave-ovens"}
{"char_offset": [11099, 11137], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:53:49Z", "heading_path": ["Cooking with Microwave Ovens", "What containers and wraps are safe to use in the microwave oven?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "archived_copy", "paragraph_index": 95, "paragraph_text": "Any utensil labeled for microwave use.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Cooking with Microwave Ovens > What containers and wraps are safe to use in the microwave oven? > p[95]", "sha256_of_page": "a3010e76a59170a0f610de828fd54011639e828730bad77e6f455bb1c995d19f", "sha256_of_paragraph": "df580c18af4a950ec1014b9c08e4f99e96fe96b9118fd1462602264a0b59b448", "source_id": "USDA-MW", "tag": "p", "url": "http://web.archive.org/web/20261119141358/https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/cooking-microwave-ovens"}
{"char_offset": [11138, 11192], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:53:49Z", "heading_path": ["Cooking with Microwave Ovens", "What containers and wraps are safe to use in the microwave oven?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "archived_copy", "paragraph_index": 96, "paragraph_text": "Heatproof glass (such as Pyrex, Anchor Hocking, etc.).", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Cooking with Microwave Ovens > What containers and wraps are safe to use in the microwave oven? > p[96]", "sha256_of_page": "a3010e76a59170a0f610de828fd54011639e828730bad77e6f455bb1c995d19f", "sha256_of_paragraph": "c1970473f1795932463a88fbb8c9de4055f937cb8dab9d9bde6d28ff8d6072d9", "source_id": "USDA-MW", "tag": "p", "url": "http://web.archive.org/web/20261119141358/https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/cooking-microwave-ovens"}
{"char_offset": [11193, 11230], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:53:49Z", "heading_path": ["Cooking with Microwave Ovens", "What containers and wraps are safe to use in the microwave oven?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "archived_copy", "paragraph_index": 97, "paragraph_text": "Glass-ceramic (such as Corning Ware).", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Cooking with Microwave Ovens > What containers and wraps are safe to use in the microwave oven? > p[97]", "sha256_of_page": "a3010e76a59170a0f610de828fd54011639e828730bad77e6f455bb1c995d19f", "sha256_of_paragraph": "e543cfe5a8893bb929551f149ae057d2c6346a9bcf297a64dca7fd55d599873a", "source_id": "USDA-MW", "tag": "p", "url": "http://web.archive.org/web/20261119141358/https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/cooking-microwave-ovens"}
{"char_offset": [11231, 11352], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:53:49Z", "heading_path": ["Cooking with Microwave Ovens", "What containers and wraps are safe to use in the microwave oven?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "archived_copy", "paragraph_index": 98, "paragraph_text": "Baskets (straw and wood) for quick warm-ups of rolls or bread. Line the basket with napkins to absorb moisture from food.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Cooking with Microwave Ovens > What containers and wraps are safe to use in the microwave oven? > p[98]", "sha256_of_page": "a3010e76a59170a0f610de828fd54011639e828730bad77e6f455bb1c995d19f", "sha256_of_paragraph": "8c24c33c1db4783e2fabe941e168af3cf9e2df4726b3294c0843f0ee52006bcc", "source_id": "USDA-MW", "tag": "p", "url": "http://web.archive.org/web/20261119141358/https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/cooking-microwave-ovens"}
