{"char_offset": [31, 183], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:36Z", "heading_path": ["《科学画报》"], "language": "zh-cn", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 1, "paragraph_text": "由中国科学社于1933年8月创刊，距今已有80年的历史。《科学画报》在80年的办刊历程中，形成了通俗生动、图文并茂地介绍最新科技知识，形式多样地普及科学技术的特点，对提高广大群众的科学水平，启发青年爱好科学、投身科学事业起了很大的作用，当今的不少著名学者、教授、科学家，青少年时代都曾受到它的熏陶和启发。", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "《科学画报》 > p[1]", "sha256_of_page": "3be6afbf23d5d14865af6f78c4f0b2bc62ab90672c1da3bfb508372bba94a0e5", "sha256_of_paragraph": "41302615b8599e3f096acce4eb1d6859050e9be50496046df32c7f36c8096983", "source_id": "CDSTM-BRAIN", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cdstm.cn/gallery/media/mkjx/kxhb/art/2017/art_8bbe56c7788c4a569086ef48f1b169b8.html"}
{"char_offset": [184, 235], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:36Z", "heading_path": ["《科学画报》", "大脑真的只用了10%吗？"], "language": "zh-cn", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 2, "paragraph_text": "坊间传说，爱因斯坦有一次在对记者解释他的天才时说，他发挥了全部脑的能力，而不像一般人那样只用了10%。", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "《科学画报》 > 大脑真的只用了10%吗？ > p[2]", "sha256_of_page": "3be6afbf23d5d14865af6f78c4f0b2bc62ab90672c1da3bfb508372bba94a0e5", "sha256_of_paragraph": "1359f9b190fb0147fbfd7df405c42f542c4d46508e60f6ddbc4deed33de86a85", "source_id": "CDSTM-BRAIN", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cdstm.cn/gallery/media/mkjx/kxhb/art/2017/art_8bbe56c7788c4a569086ef48f1b169b8.html"}
{"char_offset": [236, 328], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:36Z", "heading_path": ["《科学画报》", "大脑真的只用了10%吗？"], "language": "zh-cn", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 3, "paragraph_text": "后来当人们向研究爱因斯坦的档案专家索要这段话的来源时，专家们根本就找不到出处。因此，这多半只是一个杜撰的故事。另外，即使确有其事，爱因斯坦也不是一位脑科学专家，更没有做过这方面的研究。", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "《科学画报》 > 大脑真的只用了10%吗？ > p[3]", "sha256_of_page": "3be6afbf23d5d14865af6f78c4f0b2bc62ab90672c1da3bfb508372bba94a0e5", "sha256_of_paragraph": "67ce2f23907236e3bb80b2910b8191e2d45b552443ce5380e262420433b95f95", "source_id": "CDSTM-BRAIN", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cdstm.cn/gallery/media/mkjx/kxhb/art/2017/art_8bbe56c7788c4a569086ef48f1b169b8.html"}
{"char_offset": [329, 402], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:36Z", "heading_path": ["《科学画报》", "大脑真的只用了10%吗？"], "language": "zh-cn", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 4, "paragraph_text": "还有一拨人把心理学先驱、美国心理学家詹姆斯抬了出来。因为新闻记者托马斯曾写道：“哈佛大学的詹姆斯教授以前常讲，一般人只开发了其智力潜能的10%。”", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "《科学画报》 > 大脑真的只用了10%吗？ > p[4]", "sha256_of_page": "3be6afbf23d5d14865af6f78c4f0b2bc62ab90672c1da3bfb508372bba94a0e5", "sha256_of_paragraph": "70bc5c67cad864e1d13bc51ee9b84bcc5e3494649c1ad43aef6be1ce21ec9bb2", "source_id": "CDSTM-BRAIN", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cdstm.cn/gallery/media/mkjx/kxhb/art/2017/art_8bbe56c7788c4a569086ef48f1b169b8.html"}
{"char_offset": [403, 452], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:36Z", "heading_path": ["《科学画报》", "大脑真的只用了10%吗？"], "language": "zh-cn", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 5, "paragraph_text": "詹姆斯确实在20世纪初说过人有“智力潜能”，但他从来没有说过人只用了部分脑，更没有给出具体的比例。", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "《科学画报》 > 大脑真的只用了10%吗？ > p[5]", "sha256_of_page": "3be6afbf23d5d14865af6f78c4f0b2bc62ab90672c1da3bfb508372bba94a0e5", "sha256_of_paragraph": "6222fceae7ab8ade5bc21973f805201b11a7fe85ddba8b9d01ff775291cc82a6", "source_id": "CDSTM-BRAIN", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cdstm.cn/gallery/media/mkjx/kxhb/art/2017/art_8bbe56c7788c4a569086ef48f1b169b8.html"}
{"char_offset": [453, 504], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:36Z", "heading_path": ["《科学画报》", "大脑真的只用了10%吗？"], "language": "zh-cn", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 6, "paragraph_text": "爱因斯坦和詹姆斯的“言论”只是以讹传讹的开端，后来使这种说法广为人知的，是对一些脑科学研究结果的曲解。", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "《科学画报》 > 大脑真的只用了10%吗？ > p[6]", "sha256_of_page": "3be6afbf23d5d14865af6f78c4f0b2bc62ab90672c1da3bfb508372bba94a0e5", "sha256_of_paragraph": "7b942b377582b269f6d2663a55fdaeca37621240271af1d232092fc28fd602fc", "source_id": "CDSTM-BRAIN", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cdstm.cn/gallery/media/mkjx/kxhb/art/2017/art_8bbe56c7788c4a569086ef48f1b169b8.html"}
{"char_offset": [505, 669], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:36Z", "heading_path": ["《科学画报》", "大脑真的只用了10%吗？"], "language": "zh-cn", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 7, "paragraph_text": "20世纪中叶，加拿大神经外科医生彭菲尔德为了确定癫痫患者的病灶，用电极探测他们的大脑皮层，结果发现刺激皮层的有些区域能引起患者的感觉或者肌肉运动，但是也有大量区域全无反应。一些人因此误认为后者闲置不用，但现在我们已经知道，这些当时被人当作“静默皮层”的区域事实上在一些最复杂的高级功能中起到至关紧要的作用，并被称为“联合皮层”。", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "《科学画报》 > 大脑真的只用了10%吗？ > p[7]", "sha256_of_page": "3be6afbf23d5d14865af6f78c4f0b2bc62ab90672c1da3bfb508372bba94a0e5", "sha256_of_paragraph": "ed41281cfa06ad984d0a82f63817b3e3b6ac3e9e53ab774d9ad811d5c0dd569f", "source_id": "CDSTM-BRAIN", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cdstm.cn/gallery/media/mkjx/kxhb/art/2017/art_8bbe56c7788c4a569086ef48f1b169b8.html"}
{"char_offset": [670, 804], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:36Z", "heading_path": ["《科学画报》", "大脑真的只用了10%吗？"], "language": "zh-cn", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 8, "paragraph_text": "还有一种曲解源于脑中的胶质细胞要比神经细胞多10倍。过去人们对胶质细胞的作用不清楚，就误认为脑细胞中只有十分之一在起作用。现在研究发现，胶质细胞不仅在支持神经细胞、清除废物和有害物质、提供营养等方面起作用，而且它们还会调制神经细胞的活动，直接或间接地对信息处理起作用。", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "《科学画报》 > 大脑真的只用了10%吗？ > p[8]", "sha256_of_page": "3be6afbf23d5d14865af6f78c4f0b2bc62ab90672c1da3bfb508372bba94a0e5", "sha256_of_paragraph": "e7fff8b30eff8cefc981d43180581df7e7dd97c656e7b28707fcfe041ecdc8f7", "source_id": "CDSTM-BRAIN", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cdstm.cn/gallery/media/mkjx/kxhb/art/2017/art_8bbe56c7788c4a569086ef48f1b169b8.html"}
{"char_offset": [805, 948], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:36Z", "heading_path": ["《科学画报》", "大脑真的只用了10%吗？"], "language": "zh-cn", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 9, "paragraph_text": "脑成像技术揭示，即使当受试者一无所思时，整个脑也在不断地活动。从进化的角度来看，“只用10%的脑”也是难以置信的。要知道，脑是人体中“开销”最大的器官，它虽然只占整个体重的2%，但它所消耗的能量即使在安静时也要占到整个能量消耗的20%，如果有90%的脑无所事事，这样的进化是不被允许的。", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "《科学画报》 > 大脑真的只用了10%吗？ > p[9]", "sha256_of_page": "3be6afbf23d5d14865af6f78c4f0b2bc62ab90672c1da3bfb508372bba94a0e5", "sha256_of_paragraph": "9206f05804210a5c239dc4a7ccc1dca0dd80c46ecbd6bc32cd8b9c7f09cb2b0a", "source_id": "CDSTM-BRAIN", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cdstm.cn/gallery/media/mkjx/kxhb/art/2017/art_8bbe56c7788c4a569086ef48f1b169b8.html"}
{"char_offset": [0, 81], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:39Z", "heading_path": [], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 0, "paragraph_text": "How to Feast for Your Eyes | NIH Intramural Research Program Skip to main content", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "p[0]", "sha256_of_page": "6c954cd8885953ab2372eee9deed2f55ea3c6a849e4b000dea194802154d2aac", "sha256_of_paragraph": "f344ae33e78d3ddbe2d3ecdbd780c8c06f0ab50ab52a38b3a5b604e2919ada1a", "source_id": "NIH-NEI-CARROT", "tag": "p", "url": "https://irp.nih.gov/our-research/research-in-action/how-to-feast-for-your-eyes"}
{"char_offset": [82, 169], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:39Z", "heading_path": ["How to Feast for Your Eyes"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 1, "paragraph_text": "Emily Chew has a vision to reduce the risk of eye disease by filling gaps in nutrition.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "How to Feast for Your Eyes > p[1]", "sha256_of_page": "6c954cd8885953ab2372eee9deed2f55ea3c6a849e4b000dea194802154d2aac", "sha256_of_paragraph": "4995c747a4174de60c9df9567f89ba957525c9b44568170e969c30722293c10d", "source_id": "NIH-NEI-CARROT", "tag": "p", "url": "https://irp.nih.gov/our-research/research-in-action/how-to-feast-for-your-eyes"}
{"char_offset": [170, 447], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:39Z", "heading_path": ["How to Feast for Your Eyes"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 2, "paragraph_text": "It is often said that you are what you eat, but according to Dr. Emily Chew, you also see how you eat. Whether it’s building strong bones, fueling muscles, or regulating gut function, our bodies need the proper nutrients to stay healthy — and our visual system is no exception.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "How to Feast for Your Eyes > p[2]", "sha256_of_page": "6c954cd8885953ab2372eee9deed2f55ea3c6a849e4b000dea194802154d2aac", "sha256_of_paragraph": "c0235b3d42701295190320ea404940d082dbc4cc3474b866881b454b151181a9", "source_id": "NIH-NEI-CARROT", "tag": "p", "url": "https://irp.nih.gov/our-research/research-in-action/how-to-feast-for-your-eyes"}
{"char_offset": [448, 873], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:39Z", "heading_path": ["How to Feast for Your Eyes"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 3, "paragraph_text": "As the director of the Division of Epidemiology and Clinical Applications and the deputy clinical director of NIH’s National Eye Institute (NEI), Dr. Chew focuses on the retina, the layer of tissue lining the back inside wall of the eye that converts incoming light into electrical signals that the brain interprets to produce our sense of sight. To develop normally and work well, the retina must have the right ingredients.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "How to Feast for Your Eyes > p[3]", "sha256_of_page": "6c954cd8885953ab2372eee9deed2f55ea3c6a849e4b000dea194802154d2aac", "sha256_of_paragraph": "03dae2ea930592dedf1856b4259347dc51259a786d45ab4156cb5d306205678c", "source_id": "NIH-NEI-CARROT", "tag": "p", "url": "https://irp.nih.gov/our-research/research-in-action/how-to-feast-for-your-eyes"}
{"char_offset": [874, 971], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:39Z", "heading_path": ["How to Feast for Your Eyes"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 4, "paragraph_text": "“Our retinas need high concentrations of nutrients that we can get from our diet,” says Dr. Chew.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "How to Feast for Your Eyes > p[4]", "sha256_of_page": "6c954cd8885953ab2372eee9deed2f55ea3c6a849e4b000dea194802154d2aac", "sha256_of_paragraph": "9b0e16d1069cbb85f8983343d72fc52078bbcdbb3810141806c047fc3914a098", "source_id": "NIH-NEI-CARROT", "tag": "p", "url": "https://irp.nih.gov/our-research/research-in-action/how-to-feast-for-your-eyes"}
{"char_offset": [972, 1400], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:39Z", "heading_path": ["How to Feast for Your Eyes"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 5, "paragraph_text": "The idea that what we eat affects our eyes is far from new. The ancient Greeks, for example, took to eating raw beef liver to treat night blindness. Similarly, carrots and bilberries — a close cousin to blueberries — gained a reputation for improving night vision during World War II when rumors spread that British Royal Air Force pilots increased their success in night bombing missions after eating a diet rich in both foods.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "How to Feast for Your Eyes > p[5]", "sha256_of_page": "6c954cd8885953ab2372eee9deed2f55ea3c6a849e4b000dea194802154d2aac", "sha256_of_paragraph": "7eb4823c4a8babe50039dc883bbcc7c1edf8d71de20aed41e0932e3a3d36fe60", "source_id": "NIH-NEI-CARROT", "tag": "p", "url": "https://irp.nih.gov/our-research/research-in-action/how-to-feast-for-your-eyes"}
{"char_offset": [1401, 1705], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:39Z", "heading_path": ["How to Feast for Your Eyes"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 6, "paragraph_text": "Unfortunately, no amounts of liver, carrots, or bilberries will give you owl-like night vision. Nevertheless, all three foods are abundant in vitamin A, which is essential for overall eye health, as seen in children with poor vision and a vitamin A deficiency whose sight improves with daily supplements.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "How to Feast for Your Eyes > p[6]", "sha256_of_page": "6c954cd8885953ab2372eee9deed2f55ea3c6a849e4b000dea194802154d2aac", "sha256_of_paragraph": "2ff007c0351a94fbdbdf4142e99110729bed8b4e4dd4cc6c6140f698819753a2", "source_id": "NIH-NEI-CARROT", "tag": "p", "url": "https://irp.nih.gov/our-research/research-in-action/how-to-feast-for-your-eyes"}
{"char_offset": [1706, 2021], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:39Z", "heading_path": ["How to Feast for Your Eyes"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 7, "paragraph_text": "Dr. Chew suspects that other eye-related conditions could also be the result of nutrient deficits in people’s diets. She is taking a closer look at how these nutritional gaps contribute to vision loss, setting her sights on slowing the progression of retinal diseases such as age-related macular degeneration (AMD).", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "How to Feast for Your Eyes > p[7]", "sha256_of_page": "6c954cd8885953ab2372eee9deed2f55ea3c6a849e4b000dea194802154d2aac", "sha256_of_paragraph": "2a5b0cad72e609d0f38d4afa47be34927450fbea923478ff90bd6195e9a80f08", "source_id": "NIH-NEI-CARROT", "tag": "p", "url": "https://irp.nih.gov/our-research/research-in-action/how-to-feast-for-your-eyes"}
{"char_offset": [2022, 2376], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:39Z", "heading_path": ["How to Feast for Your Eyes"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 8, "paragraph_text": "“Patients with AMD usually have problems reading or adjusting their vision in dimly lit conditions, or they’ll have warped vision, like seeing crooked telephone poles or window frames,” says Dr. Chew. These distortions result from deterioration in the center part of the retina, called the macula, and can eventually rob patients of clear central vision.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "How to Feast for Your Eyes > p[8]", "sha256_of_page": "6c954cd8885953ab2372eee9deed2f55ea3c6a849e4b000dea194802154d2aac", "sha256_of_paragraph": "ff4e5f5bf3d2f3055a2bc0160af1ca2cb0200d9f3f797c5c75b87e79ef27e43d", "source_id": "NIH-NEI-CARROT", "tag": "p", "url": "https://irp.nih.gov/our-research/research-in-action/how-to-feast-for-your-eyes"}
{"char_offset": [2377, 2512], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:39Z", "heading_path": ["How to Feast for Your Eyes"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 9, "paragraph_text": "AMD is an eye disease that causes blurriness or spots in the center of your vision. Video courtesy of the National Eye Institute (NEI).", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "How to Feast for Your Eyes > p[9]", "sha256_of_page": "6c954cd8885953ab2372eee9deed2f55ea3c6a849e4b000dea194802154d2aac", "sha256_of_paragraph": "dab52f90917862a481501eddc25a707de704c87931b08397ef7294632e49e36f", "source_id": "NIH-NEI-CARROT", "tag": "p", "url": "https://irp.nih.gov/our-research/research-in-action/how-to-feast-for-your-eyes"}
{"char_offset": [2513, 3032], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:39Z", "heading_path": ["How to Feast for Your Eyes"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 10, "paragraph_text": "In a long-term study of nearly 4,000 people with AMD between the ages of 55 and 80, Dr. Chew and her collaborators tested how a dietary supplement affected the disease’s advancement. The supplement contained antioxidants in the form of vitamins C and E along with minerals like zinc and copper and was called AREDS after the name of the study — Age-Related Eye Disease Study. After five years, the researchers found that people with early-stage AMD who took AREDS had a 25 percent lower risk of developing advanced AMD.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "How to Feast for Your Eyes > p[10]", "sha256_of_page": "6c954cd8885953ab2372eee9deed2f55ea3c6a849e4b000dea194802154d2aac", "sha256_of_paragraph": "89d57b6df9e716d2840bdb2be1feb7a2b47b05b00f9184c06bfe5d4b2630ed6f", "source_id": "NIH-NEI-CARROT", "tag": "p", "url": "https://irp.nih.gov/our-research/research-in-action/how-to-feast-for-your-eyes"}
{"char_offset": [3033, 3201], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:39Z", "heading_path": ["How to Feast for Your Eyes"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 11, "paragraph_text": "“It turned out to be a secondary prevention, in that you have to be at a certain early stage of the disease in order to benefit from the supplement,” Dr. Chew explains.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "How to Feast for Your Eyes > p[11]", "sha256_of_page": "6c954cd8885953ab2372eee9deed2f55ea3c6a849e4b000dea194802154d2aac", "sha256_of_paragraph": "3bd84a8687045cc3797e26752783b0e945a29e20e9500af6577790469d8c1486", "source_id": "NIH-NEI-CARROT", "tag": "p", "url": "https://irp.nih.gov/our-research/research-in-action/how-to-feast-for-your-eyes"}
{"char_offset": [3202, 3385], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:39Z", "heading_path": ["How to Feast for Your Eyes"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 12, "paragraph_text": "Macular degeneration is characterized by the appearance of small yellow or white deposits, called drusen, on the retina. These deposits can accumulate and impair clear central vision.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "How to Feast for Your Eyes > p[12]", "sha256_of_page": "6c954cd8885953ab2372eee9deed2f55ea3c6a849e4b000dea194802154d2aac", "sha256_of_paragraph": "e696bb8e93b395fd93c9befbc9df40658ccdfd6a1643de15bd2924bfa8dfa683", "source_id": "NIH-NEI-CARROT", "tag": "p", "url": "https://irp.nih.gov/our-research/research-in-action/how-to-feast-for-your-eyes"}
{"char_offset": [3386, 3896], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:39Z", "heading_path": ["How to Feast for Your Eyes"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 13, "paragraph_text": "From close analysis of participants’ diets over the course of the study, the researchers also learned that people who ate more leafy green vegetables and fish tended to have a lower risk of developing advanced AMD. Encouraged by this data, Dr. Chew and her team organized a second AREDS study testing whether adding lutein and zeaxanthin — compounds found in the leaves of collard greens, spinach, and kale — along with the omega-3 fatty acids of oily fish such as salmon, could further slow the course of AMD.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "How to Feast for Your Eyes > p[13]", "sha256_of_page": "6c954cd8885953ab2372eee9deed2f55ea3c6a849e4b000dea194802154d2aac", "sha256_of_paragraph": "e4a575c279b7e0af88cd66859f844f538d8cd371fb681dd626e64cfe4a976a6e", "source_id": "NIH-NEI-CARROT", "tag": "p", "url": "https://irp.nih.gov/our-research/research-in-action/how-to-feast-for-your-eyes"}
{"char_offset": [3897, 4376], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:39Z", "heading_path": ["How to Feast for Your Eyes"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 14, "paragraph_text": "The researchers concluded that omega-3s didn’t have a significant effect, but lutein and zeaxanthin were somewhat effective, especially as substitutes for beta-carotene, another plant-derived compound used in the original AREDS supplement that was found to increase the risk of cancer by independent studies. The AREDS2 supplement was approximately 20 percent more beneficial for reducing progression to advanced AMD compared to the original formula that contained beta-carotene.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "How to Feast for Your Eyes > p[14]", "sha256_of_page": "6c954cd8885953ab2372eee9deed2f55ea3c6a849e4b000dea194802154d2aac", "sha256_of_paragraph": "02a5109f5b2d02b4b64ca7b06d5bf996cb8c083e885536ff0ec7b70abdf171e8", "source_id": "NIH-NEI-CARROT", "tag": "p", "url": "https://irp.nih.gov/our-research/research-in-action/how-to-feast-for-your-eyes"}
{"char_offset": [4377, 4822], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:39Z", "heading_path": ["How to Feast for Your Eyes"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 15, "paragraph_text": "AREDS2 is now available in stores, but Dr. Chew emphasizes that people should not take it unless an eye specialist has spotted the firsts signs of disease in their retinas. Instead, she recommends focusing on getting the same nutrients directly from food. Trends in both AREDS studies suggest that a Mediterranean diet, which includes lots of vegetables, whole grains, fish, and olive oil, is better at staving off AMD than the supplement alone.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "How to Feast for Your Eyes > p[15]", "sha256_of_page": "6c954cd8885953ab2372eee9deed2f55ea3c6a849e4b000dea194802154d2aac", "sha256_of_paragraph": "ad8525e0dd9a9806a58c4386f5bc869b36a9319dcbfa067421bd93467719f174", "source_id": "NIH-NEI-CARROT", "tag": "p", "url": "https://irp.nih.gov/our-research/research-in-action/how-to-feast-for-your-eyes"}
{"char_offset": [4823, 4952], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:39Z", "heading_path": ["How to Feast for Your Eyes"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 16, "paragraph_text": "“This is just observational data — but it’s compelling that people who eat well avert the development of disease,” says Dr. Chew.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "How to Feast for Your Eyes > p[16]", "sha256_of_page": "6c954cd8885953ab2372eee9deed2f55ea3c6a849e4b000dea194802154d2aac", "sha256_of_paragraph": "fdb190adf756524cdc0616298448050b079b8001429af2cdcc240f48ab6f2c48", "source_id": "NIH-NEI-CARROT", "tag": "p", "url": "https://irp.nih.gov/our-research/research-in-action/how-to-feast-for-your-eyes"}
{"char_offset": [4953, 5073], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:39Z", "heading_path": ["How to Feast for Your Eyes"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 17, "paragraph_text": "Dr. Chew recommends a Mediterranean diet consisting of fish, olive oil, vegetables, and grains to lower the risk of AMD.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "How to Feast for Your Eyes > p[17]", "sha256_of_page": "6c954cd8885953ab2372eee9deed2f55ea3c6a849e4b000dea194802154d2aac", "sha256_of_paragraph": "411f1c983934a8e30cf81830018d36ca1487a87098043577e247f5bd43a39902", "source_id": "NIH-NEI-CARROT", "tag": "p", "url": "https://irp.nih.gov/our-research/research-in-action/how-to-feast-for-your-eyes"}
{"char_offset": [5074, 5886], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:39Z", "heading_path": ["How to Feast for Your Eyes"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 18, "paragraph_text": "Aside from nutritional insights, the AREDS studies are also spurring new directions in the study of macular degeneration that take advantage of artificial intelligence (AI) and machine learning. Her team is currently working with Zhiyong Lu, Ph.D., from NIH’s National Center for Biotechnology Information (NCBI) to process the trove of retinal images collected from participants over the course of the studies. Together, they are attempting to create a computer algorithm that can help detect AMD and predict its progression. The project involves training a computer using retinal images from people who went on to develop advanced AMD and others from people who didn’t in the hopes that the computer can learn to use a baseline scan of the retina to identify patients who are more likely to develop severe AMD.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "How to Feast for Your Eyes > p[18]", "sha256_of_page": "6c954cd8885953ab2372eee9deed2f55ea3c6a849e4b000dea194802154d2aac", "sha256_of_paragraph": "b5c06e7273ad5e1fa2655ca9b20b73b207ae090a97dedee68f2c39cf90f1c0e1", "source_id": "NIH-NEI-CARROT", "tag": "p", "url": "https://irp.nih.gov/our-research/research-in-action/how-to-feast-for-your-eyes"}
{"char_offset": [5887, 6248], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:39Z", "heading_path": ["How to Feast for Your Eyes"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 19, "paragraph_text": "“It’s quite remarkable what it can do and, so far, it’s pretty darn accurate,” says Dr. Chew. In the future, she intends to include other relevant factors in the AI-based assessments, such as age, genetics, behavioral habits like smoking, and health histories, which will help paint a more comprehensive picture detailing an individual’s risk of developing AMD.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "How to Feast for Your Eyes > p[19]", "sha256_of_page": "6c954cd8885953ab2372eee9deed2f55ea3c6a849e4b000dea194802154d2aac", "sha256_of_paragraph": "00c2f3b6eb78aa35a8d49e04ba548e1d34fe84d4436159d52cdff969d06e242d", "source_id": "NIH-NEI-CARROT", "tag": "p", "url": "https://irp.nih.gov/our-research/research-in-action/how-to-feast-for-your-eyes"}
{"char_offset": [6249, 6472], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:39Z", "heading_path": ["How to Feast for Your Eyes"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 20, "paragraph_text": "Her foray into AI represents one of the many collaborations Dr. Chew has had in her 30 years at NIH. She recognizes that fostering such interdisciplinary collaborations is a testament to the sense of camaraderie in the IRP.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "How to Feast for Your Eyes > p[20]", "sha256_of_page": "6c954cd8885953ab2372eee9deed2f55ea3c6a849e4b000dea194802154d2aac", "sha256_of_paragraph": "3ff042f6fa3f21985eb873912b83b348d3c137f22722bb77cb6828a921368fe4", "source_id": "NIH-NEI-CARROT", "tag": "p", "url": "https://irp.nih.gov/our-research/research-in-action/how-to-feast-for-your-eyes"}
{"char_offset": [6473, 6690], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:39Z", "heading_path": ["How to Feast for Your Eyes"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 21, "paragraph_text": "“Working so closely with collaborators makes the research so much more fun and so fulfilling in so many ways,” she says, “but most importantly, it benefits our patients because we can make an impact on a large scale.”", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "How to Feast for Your Eyes > p[21]", "sha256_of_page": "6c954cd8885953ab2372eee9deed2f55ea3c6a849e4b000dea194802154d2aac", "sha256_of_paragraph": "22b5310e13d2ab505d6979ed893c4f16a1616a7a967abf67276fb230fb81f448", "source_id": "NIH-NEI-CARROT", "tag": "p", "url": "https://irp.nih.gov/our-research/research-in-action/how-to-feast-for-your-eyes"}
{"char_offset": [6691, 6864], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:39Z", "heading_path": ["How to Feast for Your Eyes"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 22, "paragraph_text": "“Being at NIH has opened my eyes to so many things,” adds Dr. Chew. As her eyes continue to widen to the possibilities, it’s comforting to know she’ll be looking after ours.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "How to Feast for Your Eyes > p[22]", "sha256_of_page": "6c954cd8885953ab2372eee9deed2f55ea3c6a849e4b000dea194802154d2aac", "sha256_of_paragraph": "39dd024181de59c23419fc3f197dfd6a064c514c8403c9ad0a1ac69b2fc88b4d", "source_id": "NIH-NEI-CARROT", "tag": "p", "url": "https://irp.nih.gov/our-research/research-in-action/how-to-feast-for-your-eyes"}
{"char_offset": [6865, 7091], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:39Z", "heading_path": ["How to Feast for Your Eyes"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 23, "paragraph_text": "Emily Chew, M.D., is a senior investigator in the Clinical Trails Branch of the National Eye Institute (NEI), where she serves as Deputy Clinical Director and Director of the Division of Epidemiology and Clinical Applications.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "How to Feast for Your Eyes > p[23]", "sha256_of_page": "6c954cd8885953ab2372eee9deed2f55ea3c6a849e4b000dea194802154d2aac", "sha256_of_paragraph": "081dcea4d2a73096e1257d88d2fc9560a6d3517970d7416a634c564a982f8567", "source_id": "NIH-NEI-CARROT", "tag": "p", "url": "https://irp.nih.gov/our-research/research-in-action/how-to-feast-for-your-eyes"}
{"char_offset": [7092, 7145], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:39Z", "heading_path": ["How to Feast for Your Eyes"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 24, "paragraph_text": "This page was last updated on Wednesday, May 24, 2023", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "How to Feast for Your Eyes > p[24]", "sha256_of_page": "6c954cd8885953ab2372eee9deed2f55ea3c6a849e4b000dea194802154d2aac", "sha256_of_paragraph": "762adaf1fd5e70545eee6f34fa8bdc894e8d9ef83329d6096a402dc474c20da1", "source_id": "NIH-NEI-CARROT", "tag": "p", "url": "https://irp.nih.gov/our-research/research-in-action/how-to-feast-for-your-eyes"}
{"char_offset": [0, 57], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:43Z", "heading_path": [], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 0, "paragraph_text": "Questions and Answers on Monosodium glutamate (MSG) | FDA", "published_or_updated": "Fri, 07/17/2026 - 10:43", "selector": "p[0]", "sha256_of_page": "99b8973900d5f3fa279c194b95591d8c7ae9f05d9881cb05bbda9a712b1d8994", "sha256_of_paragraph": "68a9ab1dc532b15bc048d50d2f29437f63ee510ff20671741d728de66a9cd4be", "source_id": "FDA-MSG", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.fda.gov/food/food-additives-petitions/questions-and-answers-monosodium-glutamate-msg"}
{"char_offset": [58, 109], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:43Z", "heading_path": [], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 1, "paragraph_text": "Questions and Answers on Monosodium glutamate (MSG)", "published_or_updated": "Fri, 07/17/2026 - 10:43", "selector": "p[1]", "sha256_of_page": "99b8973900d5f3fa279c194b95591d8c7ae9f05d9881cb05bbda9a712b1d8994", "sha256_of_paragraph": "b6530fac07529f6a5f0e4ae0bf82cc88bb313025d3eb7d3af2db07145d0975a9", "source_id": "FDA-MSG", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.fda.gov/food/food-additives-petitions/questions-and-answers-monosodium-glutamate-msg"}
{"char_offset": [110, 283], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:43Z", "heading_path": [], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 2, "paragraph_text": "Monosodium glutamate (MSG) is the sodium salt of the common amino acid glutamic acid. Glutamic acid is naturally present in our bodies, and in many foods and food additives.", "published_or_updated": "Fri, 07/17/2026 - 10:43", "selector": "p[2]", "sha256_of_page": "99b8973900d5f3fa279c194b95591d8c7ae9f05d9881cb05bbda9a712b1d8994", "sha256_of_paragraph": "3f5c818b457299710e9a74177aa543e9bfa7bd0afa15253ebc52177394cf6c62", "source_id": "FDA-MSG", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.fda.gov/food/food-additives-petitions/questions-and-answers-monosodium-glutamate-msg"}
{"char_offset": [284, 784], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:43Z", "heading_path": [], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 3, "paragraph_text": "MSG occurs naturally in many foods, such as tomatoes and cheeses. People around the world have eaten glutamate-rich foods throughout history. For example, a historical dish in the Asian community is a glutamate-rich seaweed broth. In 1908, a Japanese professor named Kikunae Ikeda was able to extract glutamate from this broth and determined that glutamate provided the savory taste to the soup. Professor Ikeda then filed a patent to produce MSG and commercial production started the following year.", "published_or_updated": "Fri, 07/17/2026 - 10:43", "selector": "p[3]", "sha256_of_page": "99b8973900d5f3fa279c194b95591d8c7ae9f05d9881cb05bbda9a712b1d8994", "sha256_of_paragraph": "30293481dab5f87cac36d9cd8a146385fe37a6bb2034935d5b16d93c09520330", "source_id": "FDA-MSG", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.fda.gov/food/food-additives-petitions/questions-and-answers-monosodium-glutamate-msg"}
{"char_offset": [785, 1023], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:43Z", "heading_path": [], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 4, "paragraph_text": "Today, instead of extracting and crystallizing MSG from seaweed broth, MSG is produced by the fermentation of starch, sugar beets, sugar cane or molasses. This fermentation process is similar to that used to make yogurt, vinegar and wine.", "published_or_updated": "Fri, 07/17/2026 - 10:43", "selector": "p[4]", "sha256_of_page": "99b8973900d5f3fa279c194b95591d8c7ae9f05d9881cb05bbda9a712b1d8994", "sha256_of_paragraph": "0080b8caf8fdc4a96e3531c39c31cd30180a56c3c5b6b495d901f1b0f2251dfe", "source_id": "FDA-MSG", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.fda.gov/food/food-additives-petitions/questions-and-answers-monosodium-glutamate-msg"}
{"char_offset": [1024, 1295], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:43Z", "heading_path": [], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 5, "paragraph_text": "FDA considers the addition of MSG to foods to be “generally recognized as safe” (GRAS). Although many people identify themselves as sensitive to MSG, in studies with such individuals given MSG or a placebo, scientists have not been able to consistently trigger reactions.", "published_or_updated": "Fri, 07/17/2026 - 10:43", "selector": "p[5]", "sha256_of_page": "99b8973900d5f3fa279c194b95591d8c7ae9f05d9881cb05bbda9a712b1d8994", "sha256_of_paragraph": "d01cedfe51c22523bd99896b412f8856613e09e7f5cc376f3f599ca0b1f0f782", "source_id": "FDA-MSG", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.fda.gov/food/food-additives-petitions/questions-and-answers-monosodium-glutamate-msg"}
{"char_offset": [1296, 1350], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:43Z", "heading_path": [], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 6, "paragraph_text": "Does “glutamate” in a product mean it contains gluten?", "published_or_updated": "Fri, 07/17/2026 - 10:43", "selector": "p[6]", "sha256_of_page": "99b8973900d5f3fa279c194b95591d8c7ae9f05d9881cb05bbda9a712b1d8994", "sha256_of_paragraph": "ac1b6d0d486d2986003b20bddb1d0ac73ae0539c94d28c74472d3967c58283d5", "source_id": "FDA-MSG", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.fda.gov/food/food-additives-petitions/questions-and-answers-monosodium-glutamate-msg"}
{"char_offset": [1351, 1533], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:43Z", "heading_path": [], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 7, "paragraph_text": "No—glutamate or glutamic acid have nothing to do with gluten. A person with Celiac disease may react to the wheat that may be present in soy sauce, but not to the MSG in the product.", "published_or_updated": "Fri, 07/17/2026 - 10:43", "selector": "p[7]", "sha256_of_page": "99b8973900d5f3fa279c194b95591d8c7ae9f05d9881cb05bbda9a712b1d8994", "sha256_of_paragraph": "4a85d11ee655fdd8a088732b92a7df7049e4640d81eb8c8f5300d3ff0888db9b", "source_id": "FDA-MSG", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.fda.gov/food/food-additives-petitions/questions-and-answers-monosodium-glutamate-msg"}
{"char_offset": [1534, 1590], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:43Z", "heading_path": [], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 8, "paragraph_text": "What’s the difference between MSG and glutamate in food?", "published_or_updated": "Fri, 07/17/2026 - 10:43", "selector": "p[8]", "sha256_of_page": "99b8973900d5f3fa279c194b95591d8c7ae9f05d9881cb05bbda9a712b1d8994", "sha256_of_paragraph": "524864e0e43ae8b09c4b5b923cfc2ae85193746d6a88ca760f97b164370a0acd", "source_id": "FDA-MSG", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.fda.gov/food/food-additives-petitions/questions-and-answers-monosodium-glutamate-msg"}
{"char_offset": [1591, 1929], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:43Z", "heading_path": [], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 9, "paragraph_text": "Proteins are made up of amino acids, including glutamic acid. When proteins are digested, they release free glutamate, which is chemically the same as the glutamate in MSG. An average adult consumes approximately 13 grams of glutamate each day from the protein in food, while intake of added MSG is estimated at around 0.55 grams per day.", "published_or_updated": "Fri, 07/17/2026 - 10:43", "selector": "p[9]", "sha256_of_page": "99b8973900d5f3fa279c194b95591d8c7ae9f05d9881cb05bbda9a712b1d8994", "sha256_of_paragraph": "ad1ad8cf9b68436e57e352f4f5dc40c1f6a6b8d24059bf59af6640e582216522", "source_id": "FDA-MSG", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.fda.gov/food/food-additives-petitions/questions-and-answers-monosodium-glutamate-msg"}
{"char_offset": [1930, 1969], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:43Z", "heading_path": [], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 10, "paragraph_text": "How can I know if my food contains MSG?", "published_or_updated": "Fri, 07/17/2026 - 10:43", "selector": "p[10]", "sha256_of_page": "99b8973900d5f3fa279c194b95591d8c7ae9f05d9881cb05bbda9a712b1d8994", "sha256_of_paragraph": "2fc7e7b8d5ab0e1cb45548366d7016a1015124a3578ed43dfa1844d1824b36df", "source_id": "FDA-MSG", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.fda.gov/food/food-additives-petitions/questions-and-answers-monosodium-glutamate-msg"}
{"char_offset": [1970, 2494], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:43Z", "heading_path": [], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 11, "paragraph_text": "FDA requires that foods containing added MSG list it in the ingredient panel on the packaging as “monosodium glutamate.” However, free glutamate and sodium occur together naturally in ingredients such as hydrolyzed vegetable protein, autolyzed yeast, hydrolyzed yeast, yeast extract, soy extracts, and protein isolate, as well as in tomatoes and cheeses. While FDA requires that these ingredients be listed on the ingredient panel, the agency does not require the label to also specify that they naturally contain glutamate.", "published_or_updated": "Fri, 07/17/2026 - 10:43", "selector": "p[11]", "sha256_of_page": "99b8973900d5f3fa279c194b95591d8c7ae9f05d9881cb05bbda9a712b1d8994", "sha256_of_paragraph": "d0782d1407d5653e76b0f570d24a615ece38e0564686cf856e809f2d973d4d1e", "source_id": "FDA-MSG", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.fda.gov/food/food-additives-petitions/questions-and-answers-monosodium-glutamate-msg"}
{"char_offset": [2495, 2790], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:43Z", "heading_path": [], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 12, "paragraph_text": "Foods with ingredients naturally containing free glutamate, such as hydrolyzed vegetable protein, autolyzed yeast, hydrolyzed yeast, yeast extract, soy extracts, and protein isolate, cannot claim “No MSG” or “No added MSG” on their packaging. MSG also cannot be listed as “spices and flavoring.”", "published_or_updated": "Fri, 07/17/2026 - 10:43", "selector": "p[12]", "sha256_of_page": "99b8973900d5f3fa279c194b95591d8c7ae9f05d9881cb05bbda9a712b1d8994", "sha256_of_paragraph": "9668e714fe0b9f368c4fcfe8268e2284e1f092f72592f4bbeadfee89de242891", "source_id": "FDA-MSG", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.fda.gov/food/food-additives-petitions/questions-and-answers-monosodium-glutamate-msg"}
{"char_offset": [2791, 2935], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:43Z", "heading_path": [], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 13, "paragraph_text": "Other salts of glutamic acid, such as monopotassium glutamate and monoammonium glutamate, similarly must be specifically declared on the labels.", "published_or_updated": "Fri, 07/17/2026 - 10:43", "selector": "p[13]", "sha256_of_page": "99b8973900d5f3fa279c194b95591d8c7ae9f05d9881cb05bbda9a712b1d8994", "sha256_of_paragraph": "46a5fc104ff3fb5781200aa052a59d0984fd7205285fe1e305be266ffc192167", "source_id": "FDA-MSG", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.fda.gov/food/food-additives-petitions/questions-and-answers-monosodium-glutamate-msg"}
{"char_offset": [2936, 2999], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:43Z", "heading_path": [], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 14, "paragraph_text": "Has FDA received any adverse event reports associated with MSG?", "published_or_updated": "Fri, 07/17/2026 - 10:43", "selector": "p[14]", "sha256_of_page": "99b8973900d5f3fa279c194b95591d8c7ae9f05d9881cb05bbda9a712b1d8994", "sha256_of_paragraph": "26ed0c56d0bbbd45460c2a4a1f45bf637f9a7b0d0fe2052945fc201da1e63f40", "source_id": "FDA-MSG", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.fda.gov/food/food-additives-petitions/questions-and-answers-monosodium-glutamate-msg"}
{"char_offset": [3000, 3196], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:43Z", "heading_path": [], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 15, "paragraph_text": "Over the years, FDA has received reports of symptoms such as headache and nausea after eating foods containing MSG. However, we were never able to confirm that the MSG caused the reported effects.", "published_or_updated": "Fri, 07/17/2026 - 10:43", "selector": "p[15]", "sha256_of_page": "99b8973900d5f3fa279c194b95591d8c7ae9f05d9881cb05bbda9a712b1d8994", "sha256_of_paragraph": "457ef21e6f92217b9fb8ec4caedb61a9adddefa936c08f19ee7d6486a41141c0", "source_id": "FDA-MSG", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.fda.gov/food/food-additives-petitions/questions-and-answers-monosodium-glutamate-msg"}
{"char_offset": [3197, 3853], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:43Z", "heading_path": [], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 16, "paragraph_text": "These adverse event reports helped trigger FDA to ask the independent scientific group Federation of American Societies for Experimental Biology (FASEB) to examine the safety of MSG in the 1990s. FASEB’s report concluded that MSG is safe. The FASEB report identified some short-term, transient, and generally mild symptoms, such as headache, numbness, flushing, tingling, palpitations, and drowsiness that may occur in some sensitive individuals who consume 3 grams or more of MSG without food. However, a typical serving of a food with added MSG contains less than 0.5 grams of MSG. Consuming more than 3 grams of MSG without food at one time is unlikely.", "published_or_updated": "Fri, 07/17/2026 - 10:43", "selector": "p[16]", "sha256_of_page": "99b8973900d5f3fa279c194b95591d8c7ae9f05d9881cb05bbda9a712b1d8994", "sha256_of_paragraph": "54b2b918ce484dd4ec7227e4a575b967afe8d9a6355cf400b4cded232b6009c7", "source_id": "FDA-MSG", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.fda.gov/food/food-additives-petitions/questions-and-answers-monosodium-glutamate-msg"}
{"char_offset": [0, 65], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:46Z", "heading_path": [], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 0, "paragraph_text": "Common Cancer Myths and Misconceptions - NCI Skip to main content", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "p[0]", "sha256_of_page": "0b9ee7c0628bf8b27350590e0d13d1c5bb0663e3a3293cc94ad3d0368d5720b6", "sha256_of_paragraph": "e5e3e8a8d9d42e00a660e38cc6939b42f095589df3e5b046c5ddf4fcdf348794", "source_id": "NCI-MYTHS", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/causes-prevention/risk/myths"}
{"char_offset": [66, 259], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:46Z", "heading_path": [], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 1, "paragraph_text": "Per Executive Order 14434, federal agencies are now using \"Super Intelligence\" (SI) in place of \"Artificial Intelligence\" (AI). You may see both terms across NIH content during this transition.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "p[1]", "sha256_of_page": "0b9ee7c0628bf8b27350590e0d13d1c5bb0663e3a3293cc94ad3d0368d5720b6", "sha256_of_paragraph": "0018e92cdf298b608f11f10dcf0a4383a34209122e7e963be7da5dad80ba61d1", "source_id": "NCI-MYTHS", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/causes-prevention/risk/myths"}
{"char_offset": [260, 645], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:46Z", "heading_path": ["Common Cancer Myths and Misconceptions"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 2, "paragraph_text": "Certain popular ideas about how cancer starts and spreads—though scientifically wrong—can seem to make sense, especially when those ideas are rooted in old theories. But wrong ideas about cancer can lead to needless worry and even hinder good prevention and treatment decisions. This page provides the latest science-based information about some common cancer myths and misconceptions.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Common Cancer Myths and Misconceptions > p[2]", "sha256_of_page": "0b9ee7c0628bf8b27350590e0d13d1c5bb0663e3a3293cc94ad3d0368d5720b6", "sha256_of_paragraph": "4da5e82f918331f22dbc732f462ee2cb404c819e82421bf332ef020a00459507", "source_id": "NCI-MYTHS", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/causes-prevention/risk/myths"}
{"char_offset": [646, 1025], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:46Z", "heading_path": ["Common Cancer Myths and Misconceptions", "Is cancer a death sentence?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 3, "paragraph_text": "In the United States, the likelihood of dying from cancer has dropped steadily since the 1990s. Five-year survival rates for some cancers, such as breast, prostate, and thyroid cancers, now are 90 percent or better. The 5-year survival rate for all cancers combined is currently about 69 percent. For more information, see the Annual Report to the Nation on the Status of Cancer.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Common Cancer Myths and Misconceptions > Is cancer a death sentence? > p[3]", "sha256_of_page": "0b9ee7c0628bf8b27350590e0d13d1c5bb0663e3a3293cc94ad3d0368d5720b6", "sha256_of_paragraph": "8bfffb55144350c31994207855c5602965ad4bbf1506cc9619a6bd9c845b7c5a", "source_id": "NCI-MYTHS", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/causes-prevention/risk/myths"}
{"char_offset": [1026, 1425], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:46Z", "heading_path": ["Common Cancer Myths and Misconceptions", "Is cancer a death sentence?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 4, "paragraph_text": "It is important to note, however, that these rates are based on data from large numbers of people. How long an individual cancer patient will live and whether he or she will die from the disease depend on many factors, including whether the cancer is slow or fast growing, how much the cancer has spread in the body, whether effective treatments are available, the person’s overall health, and more.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Common Cancer Myths and Misconceptions > Is cancer a death sentence? > p[4]", "sha256_of_page": "0b9ee7c0628bf8b27350590e0d13d1c5bb0663e3a3293cc94ad3d0368d5720b6", "sha256_of_paragraph": "a8d6c5903b90cdf8619f83bd846a364217de2a29cf1c7a2b7c86d02113d3e080", "source_id": "NCI-MYTHS", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/causes-prevention/risk/myths"}
{"char_offset": [1426, 1885], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:46Z", "heading_path": ["Common Cancer Myths and Misconceptions", "Will eating sugar make my cancer worse?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 5, "paragraph_text": "No. Although research has shown that cancer cells consume more sugar (glucose) than normal cells, no studies have shown that eating sugar will make your cancer worse or that, if you stop eating sugar, your cancer will shrink or disappear. However, a high-sugar diet may contribute to excess weight gain, and obesity is associated with an increased risk of developing several types of cancer. For more information, see the NCI fact sheet on Obesity and Cancer.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Common Cancer Myths and Misconceptions > Will eating sugar make my cancer worse? > p[5]", "sha256_of_page": "0b9ee7c0628bf8b27350590e0d13d1c5bb0663e3a3293cc94ad3d0368d5720b6", "sha256_of_paragraph": "0843b339ce42f546829646ab7c9969ee67e585de0d55164597edda189d147abb", "source_id": "NCI-MYTHS", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/causes-prevention/risk/myths"}
{"char_offset": [1886, 2442], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:46Z", "heading_path": ["Common Cancer Myths and Misconceptions", "Is cancer contagious?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 6, "paragraph_text": "In general, no. Cancer is not a contagious disease that easily spreads from person to person. The only situation in which cancer can spread from one person to another is in the case of organ or tissue transplantation. A person who receives an organ or tissue from a donor who had cancer in the past may be at increased risk of developing a transplant-related cancer in the future. However, that risk is extremely low—about two cases of cancer per 10,000 organ transplants. Doctors avoid the use of organs or tissue from donors who have a history of cancer.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Common Cancer Myths and Misconceptions > Is cancer contagious? > p[6]", "sha256_of_page": "0b9ee7c0628bf8b27350590e0d13d1c5bb0663e3a3293cc94ad3d0368d5720b6", "sha256_of_paragraph": "845b4d13adca3d50811190c5a6a10d2498900d3d760d67e83c40981d12d08ed7", "source_id": "NCI-MYTHS", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/causes-prevention/risk/myths"}
{"char_offset": [2443, 2908], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:46Z", "heading_path": ["Common Cancer Myths and Misconceptions", "Is cancer contagious?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 7, "paragraph_text": "In some people, cancers may be caused by certain viruses (some types of human papillomavirus, or HPV, for example) and bacteria (such as Helicobacter pylori). While a virus or bacterium can spread from person to person, the cancers they sometimes cause cannot spread from person to person. For more information about cancer-causing viruses and bacteria, see the NCI fact sheet on Helicobacter pylori and Cancer and our pages on HPV and Cancer and Infectious Agents.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Common Cancer Myths and Misconceptions > Is cancer contagious? > p[7]", "sha256_of_page": "0b9ee7c0628bf8b27350590e0d13d1c5bb0663e3a3293cc94ad3d0368d5720b6", "sha256_of_paragraph": "d38ef93035e7d8553b86237e4e943ae082f1f72174c10f2b683324fd0340135a", "source_id": "NCI-MYTHS", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/causes-prevention/risk/myths"}
{"char_offset": [2909, 3384], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:46Z", "heading_path": ["Common Cancer Myths and Misconceptions", "Can cancer surgery or a tumor biopsy cause cancer to spread in the body?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 8, "paragraph_text": "The chance that surgery will cause cancer to spread to other parts of the body is extremely low. Following standard procedures, surgeons use special methods and take many steps to prevent cancer cells from spreading during biopsies or surgery to remove tumors. For example, if they must remove tissue from more than one area of the body, they use different surgical tools for each area. For information about how cancer spreads in the body, see our page on Metastatic Cancer.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Common Cancer Myths and Misconceptions > Can cancer surgery or a tumor biopsy cause cancer to spread in the body? > p[8]", "sha256_of_page": "0b9ee7c0628bf8b27350590e0d13d1c5bb0663e3a3293cc94ad3d0368d5720b6", "sha256_of_paragraph": "2ae4a28445e2ec57b1722f2677249237ecd7d3f16fe00c6d061dcd7a967fd06f", "source_id": "NCI-MYTHS", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/causes-prevention/risk/myths"}
{"char_offset": [3385, 3881], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:46Z", "heading_path": ["Common Cancer Myths and Misconceptions", "Does my attitude—positive or negative—determine my risk of getting or surviving cancer?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 9, "paragraph_text": "To date, there is no convincing scientific evidence that links a person’s “attitude” to their risk of developing or dying from cancer. If you have cancer, it’s normal to feel sad, angry, or discouraged sometimes and positive or upbeat at other times. People with a positive attitude may be more likely to maintain social connections and stay active, and physical activity and emotional support may help you cope with your cancer. For more information, see the NCI fact sheet on Stress and Cancer.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Common Cancer Myths and Misconceptions > Does my attitude—positive or negative—determine my risk of getting or surviving cancer? > p[9]", "sha256_of_page": "0b9ee7c0628bf8b27350590e0d13d1c5bb0663e3a3293cc94ad3d0368d5720b6", "sha256_of_paragraph": "bd4cd3f701afbb5716ac15c71cfffdebfbb8a46947e04d07689a08e78d3e4c58", "source_id": "NCI-MYTHS", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/causes-prevention/risk/myths"}
{"char_offset": [3882, 4077], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:46Z", "heading_path": ["Common Cancer Myths and Misconceptions", "Will cancer get worse if exposed to air?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 10, "paragraph_text": "No. Exposure to air will not make tumors grow faster or cause cancer to spread to other parts of the body. For information about how cancer spreads in the body, see our page on Metastatic Cancer.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Common Cancer Myths and Misconceptions > Will cancer get worse if exposed to air? > p[10]", "sha256_of_page": "0b9ee7c0628bf8b27350590e0d13d1c5bb0663e3a3293cc94ad3d0368d5720b6", "sha256_of_paragraph": "b1f1dc207ce4fe0416aecfdbb4c85444a6e8d457623fdba0cf768120a4b8bb46", "source_id": "NCI-MYTHS", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/causes-prevention/risk/myths"}
{"char_offset": [4078, 4329], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:46Z", "heading_path": ["Common Cancer Myths and Misconceptions", "Do cell phones cause cancer?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 11, "paragraph_text": "No, not according to the best studies completed so far. Cancer is caused by genetic mutations, and cell phones emit a type of low-frequency energy that does not damage genes. For more information, see the NCI fact sheet on Cell Phones and Cancer Risk.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Common Cancer Myths and Misconceptions > Do cell phones cause cancer? > p[11]", "sha256_of_page": "0b9ee7c0628bf8b27350590e0d13d1c5bb0663e3a3293cc94ad3d0368d5720b6", "sha256_of_paragraph": "79a68083d094e7a732d1dae8fa84f7a775e4f6effb3ae388c2f43855b238fa65", "source_id": "NCI-MYTHS", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/causes-prevention/risk/myths"}
{"char_offset": [4330, 4730], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:46Z", "heading_path": ["Common Cancer Myths and Misconceptions", "Do power lines cause cancer?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 12, "paragraph_text": "No, not according to the best studies completed so far. Power lines emit both electric and magnetic energy. The electric energy emitted by power lines is easily shielded or weakened by walls and other objects. The magnetic energy emitted by power lines is a low-frequency form of radiation that does not damage genes. For more information, see the NCI fact sheet on Electromagnetic Fields and Cancer.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Common Cancer Myths and Misconceptions > Do power lines cause cancer? > p[12]", "sha256_of_page": "0b9ee7c0628bf8b27350590e0d13d1c5bb0663e3a3293cc94ad3d0368d5720b6", "sha256_of_paragraph": "d231b849d1d68c8a2db307f000546e19a9a1b1f4c5d2a3c2a6ba1212807e7031", "source_id": "NCI-MYTHS", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/causes-prevention/risk/myths"}
{"char_offset": [4731, 5356], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:46Z", "heading_path": ["Common Cancer Myths and Misconceptions", "Are there herbal products that can cure cancer?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 13, "paragraph_text": "No. Although some studies suggest that alternative or complementary therapies, including some herbs, may help patients cope with the side effects of cancer treatment, no herbal products have been shown to be effective for treating cancer. In fact, some herbal products may be harmful when taken during chemotherapy or radiation therapy because they may interfere with how these treatments work. Cancer patients should talk with their doctor about any complementary and alternative medicine products—including vitamins and herbal supplements—they may be using. For more information, see Complementary and Alternative Medicine.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Common Cancer Myths and Misconceptions > Are there herbal products that can cure cancer? > p[13]", "sha256_of_page": "0b9ee7c0628bf8b27350590e0d13d1c5bb0663e3a3293cc94ad3d0368d5720b6", "sha256_of_paragraph": "3b3c97b6c72fe128647b5a2d960855de65138e0a6458fa263422e3d5e14febf3", "source_id": "NCI-MYTHS", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/causes-prevention/risk/myths"}
{"char_offset": [5357, 5729], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:46Z", "heading_path": ["Common Cancer Myths and Misconceptions", "If someone in my family has cancer, am I likely to get cancer, too?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 14, "paragraph_text": "Not necessarily. Cancer is caused by harmful changes (mutations) in genes. Only about 5 to 10 percent of cancers are caused by harmful mutations that are inherited from a person’s parents. In families with an inherited cancer-causing mutation, multiple family members will often develop the same type of cancer. These cancers are called “familial” or “hereditary” cancers.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Common Cancer Myths and Misconceptions > If someone in my family has cancer, am I likely to get cancer, too? > p[14]", "sha256_of_page": "0b9ee7c0628bf8b27350590e0d13d1c5bb0663e3a3293cc94ad3d0368d5720b6", "sha256_of_paragraph": "56e259df161658e6aa709906d8a16b28c57c9f107076d9f9e43a0c9ac31ecabb", "source_id": "NCI-MYTHS", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/causes-prevention/risk/myths"}
{"char_offset": [5730, 6166], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:46Z", "heading_path": ["Common Cancer Myths and Misconceptions", "If someone in my family has cancer, am I likely to get cancer, too?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 15, "paragraph_text": "The remaining 90 to 95 percent of cancers are caused by mutations that happen during a person’s lifetime as a natural result of aging and exposure to environmental factors, such as tobacco smoke and radiation. These cancers are called “non-hereditary” or “spontaneous” cancers. For more information about the risk of getting cancer, see the NCI fact sheet on Genetic Testing for Inherited Cancer Risk and Cancer Causes and Risk Factors.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Common Cancer Myths and Misconceptions > If someone in my family has cancer, am I likely to get cancer, too? > p[15]", "sha256_of_page": "0b9ee7c0628bf8b27350590e0d13d1c5bb0663e3a3293cc94ad3d0368d5720b6", "sha256_of_paragraph": "bb4bda91c21f21ea99c14ba427c068f170a2c4620eb4d9a8f0160425216da14e", "source_id": "NCI-MYTHS", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/causes-prevention/risk/myths"}
{"char_offset": [6167, 6692], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:46Z", "heading_path": ["Common Cancer Myths and Misconceptions", "If no one in my family has had cancer, does that mean I’m risk-free?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 16, "paragraph_text": "No. Based on the most recent data, about 39 percent of men and women will be diagnosed with cancer at some point during their lives. Most cancers are caused by genetic changes that occur throughout a person’s lifetime as a natural result of aging and exposure to environmental factors, such as tobacco smoke and radiation. Other factors, such as what kind of food you eat, how much you eat, and whether you exercise, may also influence your risk of developing cancer. For more information, see Cancer Causes and Risk Factors.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Common Cancer Myths and Misconceptions > If no one in my family has had cancer, does that mean I’m risk-free? > p[16]", "sha256_of_page": "0b9ee7c0628bf8b27350590e0d13d1c5bb0663e3a3293cc94ad3d0368d5720b6", "sha256_of_paragraph": "1f33e914cfb8a235d893fa24a7e5b319a98efdb98af46cb91d55a588106552c2", "source_id": "NCI-MYTHS", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/causes-prevention/risk/myths"}
{"char_offset": [6693, 6940], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:46Z", "heading_path": ["Common Cancer Myths and Misconceptions", "Do antiperspirants or deodorants cause breast cancer?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 17, "paragraph_text": "No. The best studies so far have found no evidence linking the chemicals typically found in antiperspirants and deodorants with changes in breast tissue. For more information, see the NCI fact sheet on Antiperspirants/Deodorants and Breast Cancer.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Common Cancer Myths and Misconceptions > Do antiperspirants or deodorants cause breast cancer? > p[17]", "sha256_of_page": "0b9ee7c0628bf8b27350590e0d13d1c5bb0663e3a3293cc94ad3d0368d5720b6", "sha256_of_paragraph": "ce01dd5a7cea148c4bb81ed89ddb1cc3f7b1e178dde07f795a6126ebb7434c2c", "source_id": "NCI-MYTHS", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/causes-prevention/risk/myths"}
{"char_offset": [0, 193], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:46:48Z", "heading_path": [], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 0, "paragraph_text": "Per Executive Order 14434, federal agencies are now using \"Super Intelligence\" (SI) in place of \"Artificial Intelligence\" (AI). You may see both terms across NIH content during this transition.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "p[0]", "sha256_of_page": "d7929aaf6b0d6c85acef366ad5755fd18f3af7bf7dd02b41062f4cdef5b6964c", "sha256_of_paragraph": "0018e92cdf298b608f11f10dcf0a4383a34209122e7e963be7da5dad80ba61d1", "source_id": "NCI-ALK", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cancer.gov/publications/dictionaries/cancer-terms/def/alkaline-diet"}
{"char_offset": [0, 82], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:49Z", "heading_path": [], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 0, "paragraph_text": "5 Tips: Natural Products for the Flu and Colds: What Does the Science Say? | NCCIH", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "p[0]", "sha256_of_page": "c2266acd99b4893b8334dcfef0ee46661e7cbf8ea624e16414a1468976ba56e1", "sha256_of_paragraph": "52df80f9845d1b294b9f6e4504f0c403f76de1c33a5494ba3ff6e3b051e38c0c", "source_id": "NCCIH-COLD", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/tips/tips-natural-products-for-the-flu-and-colds-what-does-the-science-say"}
{"char_offset": [83, 526], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:49Z", "heading_path": ["5 Tips: Natural Products for the Flu and Colds: What Does the Science Say?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 1, "paragraph_text": "Itâs that time of year againâcold and flu season. Each year, approximately 5 to 20 percent of Americans come down with the flu. Although most recover without incident, flu-related complications typically lead to at least 200,000 hospitalizations and between 12,000 and 60,000 deaths each year. Colds generally do not cause serious complications, but they are among the leading reasons for visiting a doctor and for missing school orÂ work.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "5 Tips: Natural Products for the Flu and Colds: What Does the Science Say? > p[1]", "sha256_of_page": "c2266acd99b4893b8334dcfef0ee46661e7cbf8ea624e16414a1468976ba56e1", "sha256_of_paragraph": "e88fa8671e0a941b284b6e39d4bad2633d28d02eea60fb1c16d8e1bb239d4d77", "source_id": "NCCIH-COLD", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/tips/tips-natural-products-for-the-flu-and-colds-what-does-the-science-say"}
{"char_offset": [527, 688], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:49Z", "heading_path": ["5 Tips: Natural Products for the Flu and Colds: What Does the Science Say?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 2, "paragraph_text": "Some people try natural products such as herbs or vitamins and minerals to prevent or treat these illnesses. But do they really work? What does the science say?Â", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "5 Tips: Natural Products for the Flu and Colds: What Does the Science Say? > p[2]", "sha256_of_page": "c2266acd99b4893b8334dcfef0ee46661e7cbf8ea624e16414a1468976ba56e1", "sha256_of_paragraph": "f326ba70fca4d8fce9b3f743154d3cfa408d5e462bf5b5b4fae02b60847d894e", "source_id": "NCCIH-COLD", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/tips/tips-natural-products-for-the-flu-and-colds-what-does-the-science-say"}
{"char_offset": [689, 915], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:49Z", "heading_path": ["5 Tips: Natural Products for the Flu and Colds: What Does the Science Say?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 3, "paragraph_text": "Vaccination is the best protection against getting the flu. Starting in 2010, the Federal Governmentâs Centers for Disease Control and Prevention has recommended annual flu vaccination for all people aged 6 months and older.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "5 Tips: Natural Products for the Flu and Colds: What Does the Science Say? > p[3]", "sha256_of_page": "c2266acd99b4893b8334dcfef0ee46661e7cbf8ea624e16414a1468976ba56e1", "sha256_of_paragraph": "84272f1f21614224187a8c0bf465746492c881feebc0d83a5f78be476adb479b", "source_id": "NCCIH-COLD", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/tips/tips-natural-products-for-the-flu-and-colds-what-does-the-science-say"}
{"char_offset": [916, 1017], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:49Z", "heading_path": ["5 Tips: Natural Products for the Flu and Colds: What Does the Science Say?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 4, "paragraph_text": "There is currently no strong scientific evidence that any natural product is useful against theÂ flu.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "5 Tips: Natural Products for the Flu and Colds: What Does the Science Say? > p[4]", "sha256_of_page": "c2266acd99b4893b8334dcfef0ee46661e7cbf8ea624e16414a1468976ba56e1", "sha256_of_paragraph": "53c509524b342df350d46cd21ed17e564df3c23137131fecb95642a2f527a39e", "source_id": "NCCIH-COLD", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/tips/tips-natural-products-for-the-flu-and-colds-what-does-the-science-say"}
{"char_offset": [1018, 1514], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:49Z", "heading_path": ["5 Tips: Natural Products for the Flu and Colds: What Does the Science Say?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 5, "paragraph_text": "Zinc taken orally (by mouth) may help to treat colds, but it can cause side effects and interact with medicines. Zinc is available in two formsâoral zinc (e.g., lozenges, tablets, syrup) and intranasal zinc (e.g., swabs and gels). A 2015 analysis of clinical trials found that oral zinc helps to reduce the lengthÂ of colds when taken within 24 hours after symptoms start. Intranasal zinc has been linked to a severe side effect (irreversible loss of the sense of smell) and should not be used.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "5 Tips: Natural Products for the Flu and Colds: What Does the Science Say? > p[5]", "sha256_of_page": "c2266acd99b4893b8334dcfef0ee46661e7cbf8ea624e16414a1468976ba56e1", "sha256_of_paragraph": "3aa8663a5b0be830f1bee1fe57ca1578ac06a2ff48a7c7e26bea32a871d518a4", "source_id": "NCCIH-COLD", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/tips/tips-natural-products-for-the-flu-and-colds-what-does-the-science-say"}
{"char_offset": [1515, 1810], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:49Z", "heading_path": ["5 Tips: Natural Products for the Flu and Colds: What Does the Science Say?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 6, "paragraph_text": "A note about safety: Oral zinc can cause nausea and other gastrointestinal symptoms. Long-term use of zinc, especially in high doses, can cause problems such as copper deficiency. Zinc may interact with drugs, including antibiotics and penicillamine (a drug used to treat rheumatoidÂ arthritis).", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "5 Tips: Natural Products for the Flu and Colds: What Does the Science Say? > p[6]", "sha256_of_page": "c2266acd99b4893b8334dcfef0ee46661e7cbf8ea624e16414a1468976ba56e1", "sha256_of_paragraph": "765db46aa76e880aad97de9a063aa7eb212e4da5f63d24944faf6fbcf6270d0e", "source_id": "NCCIH-COLD", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/tips/tips-natural-products-for-the-flu-and-colds-what-does-the-science-say"}
{"char_offset": [1811, 2188], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:49Z", "heading_path": ["5 Tips: Natural Products for the Flu and Colds: What Does the Science Say?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 7, "paragraph_text": "Vitamin C does not prevent colds and only slightly reduces their length and severity. A 2013 review of scientific literature found that taking vitamin C regularly did not reduce the likelihood of getting a cold but was linked to small improvements in cold symptoms. In studies in which people took vitamin C only after they got a cold, vitamin C did not improve their symptoms.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "5 Tips: Natural Products for the Flu and Colds: What Does the Science Say? > p[7]", "sha256_of_page": "c2266acd99b4893b8334dcfef0ee46661e7cbf8ea624e16414a1468976ba56e1", "sha256_of_paragraph": "7b69c6912330a759daab25a0189fc461de1a54e318f399e1146e07d3255bdfa8", "source_id": "NCCIH-COLD", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/tips/tips-natural-products-for-the-flu-and-colds-what-does-the-science-say"}
{"char_offset": [2189, 2332], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:49Z", "heading_path": ["5 Tips: Natural Products for the Flu and Colds: What Does the Science Say?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 8, "paragraph_text": "A note about safety: Vitamin C is generally considered safe; however, high doses can cause digestive disturbances such as diarrhea andÂ nausea.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "5 Tips: Natural Products for the Flu and Colds: What Does the Science Say? > p[8]", "sha256_of_page": "c2266acd99b4893b8334dcfef0ee46661e7cbf8ea624e16414a1468976ba56e1", "sha256_of_paragraph": "b63a04648dfb09e1397e485559d8d4e83769d6b73cc1295c8575216c616111fb", "source_id": "NCCIH-COLD", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/tips/tips-natural-products-for-the-flu-and-colds-what-does-the-science-say"}
{"char_offset": [2333, 2978], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:49Z", "heading_path": ["5 Tips: Natural Products for the Flu and Colds: What Does the Science Say?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 9, "paragraph_text": "Echinacea has not been proven to help prevent or treat colds. Echinacea is an herbal supplement that some people use to treat or prevent colds. Echinacea products vary widely, containing different species, parts, and preparations of the echinacea plant. Reviews of research have found limited evidence that some echinacea preparations may be useful for treating colds in adults, while other preparations did not seem to be helpful. In addition, echinacea has not been shown to reduce the number of colds that adults catch. Only a small amount of research on echinacea has been done in children, and the results of that research are inconsistent.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "5 Tips: Natural Products for the Flu and Colds: What Does the Science Say? > p[9]", "sha256_of_page": "c2266acd99b4893b8334dcfef0ee46661e7cbf8ea624e16414a1468976ba56e1", "sha256_of_paragraph": "88d545b10a83746d8957a6680c88ab6cd278a8334c5cece148a5f93c56ab54f4", "source_id": "NCCIH-COLD", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/tips/tips-natural-products-for-the-flu-and-colds-what-does-the-science-say"}
{"char_offset": [2979, 3230], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:49Z", "heading_path": ["5 Tips: Natural Products for the Flu and Colds: What Does the Science Say?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 10, "paragraph_text": "A note about safety: Few side effects have been reported in clinical trials of echinacea; however, some people may have allergic reactions.Â In one large clinical trial in children, those who took echinacea had an increased risk of developingÂ rashes.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "5 Tips: Natural Products for the Flu and Colds: What Does the Science Say? > p[10]", "sha256_of_page": "c2266acd99b4893b8334dcfef0ee46661e7cbf8ea624e16414a1468976ba56e1", "sha256_of_paragraph": "51a802add094cce0a05ec30c1abfbc44567b3963a88ab58ac78a764bcec9de06", "source_id": "NCCIH-COLD", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/tips/tips-natural-products-for-the-flu-and-colds-what-does-the-science-say"}
{"char_offset": [3231, 3805], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:49Z", "heading_path": ["5 Tips: Natural Products for the Flu and Colds: What Does the Science Say?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 11, "paragraph_text": "The evidence that probiotic supplements may help to prevent colds is weak, and little is known about their long-term safety. Probiotics are a type of âgood bacteria,â similar to the microorganisms found in the body, and may be beneficial to health. Probiotics are available as dietary supplements and yogurts, as well as other products such as suppositories and creams. Although a 2015 analysis of research indicated that probiotics might help to prevent upper respiratory tract infections, such as the common cold, the evidence is weak and the results have limitations.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "5 Tips: Natural Products for the Flu and Colds: What Does the Science Say? > p[11]", "sha256_of_page": "c2266acd99b4893b8334dcfef0ee46661e7cbf8ea624e16414a1468976ba56e1", "sha256_of_paragraph": "399a842288ca55b5895d65d69e28a80906d2889b6b7bf0f3a1307e65950a01ca", "source_id": "NCCIH-COLD", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/tips/tips-natural-products-for-the-flu-and-colds-what-does-the-science-say"}
{"char_offset": [3806, 4252], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:49:49Z", "heading_path": ["5 Tips: Natural Products for the Flu and Colds: What Does the Science Say?"], "language": "en", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 12, "paragraph_text": "A note about safety: Little is known about the effects of taking probiotics for long periods of time. Most people may be able to use probiotics without experiencing any side effectsâor with only mild gastrointestinal side effects such as gas âbut there have been some case reports of serious side effects. Probiotics should not be used by people with serious underlying health problems except with close monitoring by a health careÂ provider.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "5 Tips: Natural Products for the Flu and Colds: What Does the Science Say? > p[12]", "sha256_of_page": "c2266acd99b4893b8334dcfef0ee46661e7cbf8ea624e16414a1468976ba56e1", "sha256_of_paragraph": "ecffea94ba114c4ca76f998762c13a7809ff18ca024644462b7e1b727be1195d", "source_id": "NCCIH-COLD", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/tips/tips-natural-products-for-the-flu-and-colds-what-does-the-science-say"}
