{"char_offset": [395, 504], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:50:21Z", "heading_path": ["Sun Safety Facts", "What to know"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 6, "paragraph_text": "Most skin cancers are caused by too much exposure to ultraviolet (UV) rays. Sun safety is important all year.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Sun Safety Facts > What to know > p[6]", "sha256_of_page": "0b039247f1ac0178bfa4069dace664c34497d63945611d81130beeb49f315800", "sha256_of_paragraph": "f7202840cd2e80d2bef226b880507e4a360466062052a9f67baaa166aefbcc00", "source_id": "CDC-SUNSAFE", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/skin-cancer/sun-safety/index.html"}
{"char_offset": [505, 690], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:50:21Z", "heading_path": ["Sun Safety Facts", "Overview"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 7, "paragraph_text": "Spending time outside is a great way to be physically active and reduce stress. You can work and play outside without raising your skin cancer risk by protecting your skin from the sun.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Sun Safety Facts > Overview > p[7]", "sha256_of_page": "0b039247f1ac0178bfa4069dace664c34497d63945611d81130beeb49f315800", "sha256_of_paragraph": "c3e0e48b79f16dbbeee0a6ee1f0e331b74e80b9b5eec5d96c4d2d9a563f1587b", "source_id": "CDC-SUNSAFE", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/skin-cancer/sun-safety/index.html"}
{"char_offset": [691, 882], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:50:21Z", "heading_path": ["Sun Safety Facts", "Overview"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 8, "paragraph_text": "Most skin cancers are caused by too much exposure to ultraviolet (UV) rays. UV rays are an invisible kind of radiation that comes from the sun and tanning beds. UV rays can damage skin cells.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Sun Safety Facts > Overview > p[8]", "sha256_of_page": "0b039247f1ac0178bfa4069dace664c34497d63945611d81130beeb49f315800", "sha256_of_paragraph": "34d13612a9814f55375cfa46102bac418d74b3ed4904d77601cfeed744c13f44", "source_id": "CDC-SUNSAFE", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/skin-cancer/sun-safety/index.html"}
{"char_offset": [883, 1229], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:50:21Z", "heading_path": ["Sun Safety Facts", "Overview"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 9, "paragraph_text": "It's important to protect your skin from the sun all year, not just during the summer. The sun's UV rays can reach you on cloudy and cool days, and they reflect off of surfaces like water, cement, sand, and snow. In the continental United States, UV rays are strongest from 10 a.m. to 4 p.m. daylight saving time (9 a.m. to 3 p.m. standard time).", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Sun Safety Facts > Overview > p[9]", "sha256_of_page": "0b039247f1ac0178bfa4069dace664c34497d63945611d81130beeb49f315800", "sha256_of_paragraph": "a22763b20fd1a4001e9964d36ee8d0188608dd7956b5f0e18d642d9fa0cfa332", "source_id": "CDC-SUNSAFE", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/skin-cancer/sun-safety/index.html"}
{"char_offset": [1230, 1386], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:50:21Z", "heading_path": ["Sun Safety Facts", "Overview", "Resource"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 10, "paragraph_text": "The UV Index forecasts the strength of UV rays each day. When the UV index is 3 or higher in your area, protect your skin from too much exposure to the sun.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Sun Safety Facts > Overview > Resource > p[10]", "sha256_of_page": "0b039247f1ac0178bfa4069dace664c34497d63945611d81130beeb49f315800", "sha256_of_paragraph": "ce03798819c872b162267d8479876dd5192a81a09e715c4b5ecba99f40a8f1f7", "source_id": "CDC-SUNSAFE", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/skin-cancer/sun-safety/index.html"}
{"char_offset": [1387, 1541], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:50:21Z", "heading_path": ["Sun Safety Facts", "Overview", "What You Need to Know about Sun Safety"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 11, "paragraph_text": "Skin cancer is the most common cancer in the United States. Too much sun can cause skin cancer. This video explains how to protect your skin from the sun.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Sun Safety Facts > Overview > What You Need to Know about Sun Safety > p[11]", "sha256_of_page": "0b039247f1ac0178bfa4069dace664c34497d63945611d81130beeb49f315800", "sha256_of_paragraph": "6c8fc6cafce38b94f2537d38acd0478d676973cf7e3924c0829bac9746b40dc9", "source_id": "CDC-SUNSAFE", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/skin-cancer/sun-safety/index.html"}
{"char_offset": [1542, 1778], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:50:21Z", "heading_path": ["Sun Safety Facts", "How to protect your skin from the sun", "Shade"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 12, "paragraph_text": "You can reduce your risk of sun damage and skin cancer by staying in the shade under an umbrella, tree, or other shelter. Your best bet to protect your skin is to use sunscreen and wear protective clothing even when you're in the shade.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Sun Safety Facts > How to protect your skin from the sun > Shade > p[12]", "sha256_of_page": "0b039247f1ac0178bfa4069dace664c34497d63945611d81130beeb49f315800", "sha256_of_paragraph": "6002346b4aac9e62c45a2371cfb3d1f18766f76f3dd68bf7a9507962afac4932", "source_id": "CDC-SUNSAFE", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/skin-cancer/sun-safety/index.html"}
{"char_offset": [1779, 2155], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:50:21Z", "heading_path": ["Sun Safety Facts", "How to protect your skin from the sun", "Clothing"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 13, "paragraph_text": "When possible, wear long sleeves and long pants or skirts. If this type of clothing isn't practical, try to wear a T-shirt or a beach cover-up. Clothes made from tightly woven fabric offer the best protection. A wet T-shirt doesn't protect you as well as a dry one, and darker colors may protect more than lighter colors. Some clothing is certified to protect against UV rays.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Sun Safety Facts > How to protect your skin from the sun > Clothing > p[13]", "sha256_of_page": "0b039247f1ac0178bfa4069dace664c34497d63945611d81130beeb49f315800", "sha256_of_paragraph": "a3e45c37f1ae866b8877000e7f9eaf51f792880be93c2f5c7cd28b7d8d064a64", "source_id": "CDC-SUNSAFE", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/skin-cancer/sun-safety/index.html"}
{"char_offset": [2156, 2436], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:50:21Z", "heading_path": ["Sun Safety Facts", "How to protect your skin from the sun", "Hat"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 14, "paragraph_text": "For the most protection, wear a hat that has a brim all the way around that shades your face, your ears, and the back of your neck. A tightly woven fabric, such as canvas, works best. Avoid straw hats with holes that let sunlight through. A darker hat may offer more UV protection", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Sun Safety Facts > How to protect your skin from the sun > Hat > p[14]", "sha256_of_page": "0b039247f1ac0178bfa4069dace664c34497d63945611d81130beeb49f315800", "sha256_of_paragraph": "231a8d0945d103f0395908f98f12964d424311180e084451e71fc4b91db52e09", "source_id": "CDC-SUNSAFE", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/skin-cancer/sun-safety/index.html"}
{"char_offset": [2437, 2564], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:50:21Z", "heading_path": ["Sun Safety Facts", "How to protect your skin from the sun", "Hat"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 15, "paragraph_text": "If you wear a baseball cap, wear clothing that covers your ears and the back of your neck, use sunscreen, or stay in the shade.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Sun Safety Facts > How to protect your skin from the sun > Hat > p[15]", "sha256_of_page": "0b039247f1ac0178bfa4069dace664c34497d63945611d81130beeb49f315800", "sha256_of_paragraph": "662ca1d7ed0b1208df2cd01d74b5e62403be838fbb633326385b2ce324adefd6", "source_id": "CDC-SUNSAFE", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/skin-cancer/sun-safety/index.html"}
{"char_offset": [2565, 2642], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:50:21Z", "heading_path": ["Sun Safety Facts", "How to protect your skin from the sun", "Sunglasses"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 16, "paragraph_text": "Sunglasses protect your eyes and the skin around your eyes from sun exposure.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Sun Safety Facts > How to protect your skin from the sun > Sunglasses > p[16]", "sha256_of_page": "0b039247f1ac0178bfa4069dace664c34497d63945611d81130beeb49f315800", "sha256_of_paragraph": "c9f9e2db38cf043c2078578def40e04610abf951fc71127c8ce73159c6990e75", "source_id": "CDC-SUNSAFE", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/skin-cancer/sun-safety/index.html"}
{"char_offset": [2643, 2888], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:50:21Z", "heading_path": ["Sun Safety Facts", "How to protect your skin from the sun", "Sunglasses"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 17, "paragraph_text": "Sunglasses that block both UVA and UVB rays offer the best protection. Most sunglasses sold in the United States, regardless of cost, meet this standard. Wrap-around sunglasses work best because they block UV rays from sneaking in from the side.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Sun Safety Facts > How to protect your skin from the sun > Sunglasses > p[17]", "sha256_of_page": "0b039247f1ac0178bfa4069dace664c34497d63945611d81130beeb49f315800", "sha256_of_paragraph": "eacc3c1b12c0d13e5284d924fbbca1b993a978213a10cc5456e55d33e5a3bea6", "source_id": "CDC-SUNSAFE", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/skin-cancer/sun-safety/index.html"}
{"char_offset": [2889, 3221], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:50:21Z", "heading_path": ["Sun Safety Facts", "How to protect your skin from the sun", "Sunscreen"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 18, "paragraph_text": "Put on broad-spectrum sunscreen that filters out both UVA and UVB rays and has an SPF of 15 or higher before you go outside. Don't forget to put a thick layer on all exposed skin. Get help for hard-to-reach places like your back. And remember, sunscreen works best when combined with other options like protective clothing and hats.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Sun Safety Facts > How to protect your skin from the sun > Sunscreen > p[18]", "sha256_of_page": "0b039247f1ac0178bfa4069dace664c34497d63945611d81130beeb49f315800", "sha256_of_paragraph": "2844cbfb3cff84023ecdf9c03fef5fac40e183126ee5d6515e929bb9124c0ddf", "source_id": "CDC-SUNSAFE", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/skin-cancer/sun-safety/index.html"}
{"char_offset": [3222, 3449], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:50:21Z", "heading_path": ["Sun Safety Facts", "How to protect your skin from the sun", "Sunscreen"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 19, "paragraph_text": "Sunscreen is not recommended for babies who are 6 months old or younger. The U.S. Food and Drug Administration recommends keeping infants out of the sun during midday and using protective clothing if they have to be in the sun.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Sun Safety Facts > How to protect your skin from the sun > Sunscreen > p[19]", "sha256_of_page": "0b039247f1ac0178bfa4069dace664c34497d63945611d81130beeb49f315800", "sha256_of_paragraph": "11ea461f5cd9dd08e43b0fd34a4a50bb4926c51a33a77721add613827991c72f", "source_id": "CDC-SUNSAFE", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/skin-cancer/sun-safety/index.html"}
{"char_offset": [3450, 3684], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:50:21Z", "heading_path": ["Sun Safety Facts", "How to protect your skin from the sun", "Sunscreen"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 20, "paragraph_text": "SPF. Sunscreens are assigned a sun protection factor (SPF), which is a number that rates how well they filter out UV rays. A higher number indicates more protection. You should use a broad- spectrum sunscreen with SPF of 15 or higher.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Sun Safety Facts > How to protect your skin from the sun > Sunscreen > p[20]", "sha256_of_page": "0b039247f1ac0178bfa4069dace664c34497d63945611d81130beeb49f315800", "sha256_of_paragraph": "ec9497d1a93ce4a7eb6919837ade00436d95c6fb019d39e3cd87cbedda302529", "source_id": "CDC-SUNSAFE", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/skin-cancer/sun-safety/index.html"}
{"char_offset": [3685, 3832], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:50:21Z", "heading_path": ["Sun Safety Facts", "How to protect your skin from the sun", "Sunscreen"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 21, "paragraph_text": "Reapplication. Sunscreen wears off. Put it on again if you stay out in the sun for more than 2 hours and after swimming, sweating, or toweling off.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Sun Safety Facts > How to protect your skin from the sun > Sunscreen > p[21]", "sha256_of_page": "0b039247f1ac0178bfa4069dace664c34497d63945611d81130beeb49f315800", "sha256_of_paragraph": "2a9ca6b3c01e5496431f9c0b4c98b5f5028a2b45bf391c6f7f8d0297d5d30fd6", "source_id": "CDC-SUNSAFE", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/skin-cancer/sun-safety/index.html"}
{"char_offset": [3833, 4038], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:50:21Z", "heading_path": ["Sun Safety Facts", "How to protect your skin from the sun", "Sunscreen"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 22, "paragraph_text": "Expiration date. Check the sunscreen's expiration date. Sunscreen without an expiration date has a shelf life of no more than 3 years. Its shelf life is shorter if it has been exposed to high temperatures.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Sun Safety Facts > How to protect your skin from the sun > Sunscreen > p[22]", "sha256_of_page": "0b039247f1ac0178bfa4069dace664c34497d63945611d81130beeb49f315800", "sha256_of_paragraph": "8aae3c90a7c62040eb106ccbb62d4a69f99e7f0cdb9406c0b514ff6fc1665a9d", "source_id": "CDC-SUNSAFE", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/skin-cancer/sun-safety/index.html"}
{"char_offset": [4039, 4168], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:50:21Z", "heading_path": ["Sun Safety Facts", "How to protect your skin from the sun", "Sun Safety Tips Videos"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 23, "paragraph_text": "Dawn Holman, a behavioral scientist at CDC, explains why the following actions are important to keep your skin safe from the sun.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Sun Safety Facts > How to protect your skin from the sun > Sun Safety Tips Videos > p[23]", "sha256_of_page": "0b039247f1ac0178bfa4069dace664c34497d63945611d81130beeb49f315800", "sha256_of_paragraph": "60d3f7b5f7c037ad909819e61e722989244779076b5d779cd366d3c075f042be", "source_id": "CDC-SUNSAFE", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/skin-cancer/sun-safety/index.html"}
{"char_offset": [4169, 4225], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:50:21Z", "heading_path": ["Sun Safety Facts", "How to protect your skin from the sun", "Sun Safety Tips Videos"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 24, "paragraph_text": "Plan Activities for Morning, Late Afternoon and Evening.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Sun Safety Facts > How to protect your skin from the sun > Sun Safety Tips Videos > p[24]", "sha256_of_page": "0b039247f1ac0178bfa4069dace664c34497d63945611d81130beeb49f315800", "sha256_of_paragraph": "165c3c7a12b09a17a947a4ba57a49f4bf91ed0f07f29a7997e9ae7c6ec035e14", "source_id": "CDC-SUNSAFE", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/skin-cancer/sun-safety/index.html"}
{"char_offset": [4226, 4476], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:50:21Z", "heading_path": ["Sun Safety Facts", "Sun safety tips for schools"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 25, "paragraph_text": "Recess and other outdoor activities during the day can put students at risk if their skin isn’t protected from too much exposure to the sun. Teachers and school administrators can take steps to protect students from getting skin cancer later in life.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Sun Safety Facts > Sun safety tips for schools > p[25]", "sha256_of_page": "0b039247f1ac0178bfa4069dace664c34497d63945611d81130beeb49f315800", "sha256_of_paragraph": "867e22eba34a75704ab448b2762f56c8ff3a191f02e14e41047afc9b13451231", "source_id": "CDC-SUNSAFE", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/skin-cancer/sun-safety/index.html"}
{"char_offset": [4477, 4511], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:50:21Z", "heading_path": ["Sun Safety Facts", "Sun safety tips for schools", "Increase shade"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 26, "paragraph_text": "Provide shade structures or trees.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Sun Safety Facts > Sun safety tips for schools > Increase shade > p[26]", "sha256_of_page": "0b039247f1ac0178bfa4069dace664c34497d63945611d81130beeb49f315800", "sha256_of_paragraph": "4afe5529fa33e4c8bd37d0931b085dd93731a6a79bb92bb5f6416a70d55370f5", "source_id": "CDC-SUNSAFE", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/skin-cancer/sun-safety/index.html"}
{"char_offset": [4512, 4552], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:50:21Z", "heading_path": ["Sun Safety Facts", "Sun safety tips for schools", "Increase shade"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 27, "paragraph_text": "Move outdoor activities to shaded areas.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Sun Safety Facts > Sun safety tips for schools > Increase shade > p[27]", "sha256_of_page": "0b039247f1ac0178bfa4069dace664c34497d63945611d81130beeb49f315800", "sha256_of_paragraph": "9c87ccc9c47b0676d3faeac5bb1220ca024e0670b033c97040458d750adeb4c0", "source_id": "CDC-SUNSAFE", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/skin-cancer/sun-safety/index.html"}
{"char_offset": [4553, 4648], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:50:21Z", "heading_path": ["Sun Safety Facts", "Sun safety tips for schools", "Increase shade"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 28, "paragraph_text": "Plan for shade when developing or renovating school buildings, playgrounds, or athletic fields.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Sun Safety Facts > Sun safety tips for schools > Increase shade > p[28]", "sha256_of_page": "0b039247f1ac0178bfa4069dace664c34497d63945611d81130beeb49f315800", "sha256_of_paragraph": "9a4b336028efe9984b77a00a3a6fdbbd8f3d38f58909b10f7edfbcbb2f4bc41a", "source_id": "CDC-SUNSAFE", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/skin-cancer/sun-safety/index.html"}
{"char_offset": [4649, 4717], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:50:21Z", "heading_path": ["Sun Safety Facts", "Sun safety tips for schools", "Promote sun-safe behaviors"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 29, "paragraph_text": "Encourage students to wear hats, sunglasses, and sunscreen outdoors.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Sun Safety Facts > Sun safety tips for schools > Promote sun-safe behaviors > p[29]", "sha256_of_page": "0b039247f1ac0178bfa4069dace664c34497d63945611d81130beeb49f315800", "sha256_of_paragraph": "1e8ef0e98755c5302e8d3e5e07d61280316ed2f72e79c694aaeb7d3a5657e027", "source_id": "CDC-SUNSAFE", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/skin-cancer/sun-safety/index.html"}
{"char_offset": [4718, 4802], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:50:21Z", "heading_path": ["Sun Safety Facts", "Sun safety tips for schools", "Promote sun-safe behaviors"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 30, "paragraph_text": "Try to avoid scheduling outdoor activities during mid-day when the sun is strongest.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Sun Safety Facts > Sun safety tips for schools > Promote sun-safe behaviors > p[30]", "sha256_of_page": "0b039247f1ac0178bfa4069dace664c34497d63945611d81130beeb49f315800", "sha256_of_paragraph": "5379d3652859dd3a4749e239f0bd6a87d0cc11560e7edcc76cc309557149978d", "source_id": "CDC-SUNSAFE", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/skin-cancer/sun-safety/index.html"}
{"char_offset": [4803, 4897], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:50:21Z", "heading_path": ["Sun Safety Facts", "Sun safety tips for schools", "Promote sun-safe behaviors"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 31, "paragraph_text": "Provide breaks during outdoor activities so that students can reapply sunscreen and get water.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Sun Safety Facts > Sun safety tips for schools > Promote sun-safe behaviors > p[31]", "sha256_of_page": "0b039247f1ac0178bfa4069dace664c34497d63945611d81130beeb49f315800", "sha256_of_paragraph": "cb6292c3fc42e9402d4c3c9dc4666156a4f47e55497c294201cbb189ddade348", "source_id": "CDC-SUNSAFE", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/skin-cancer/sun-safety/index.html"}
{"char_offset": [4898, 5111], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:50:21Z", "heading_path": ["Sun Safety Facts", "Sun safety tips for schools", "Use proven educational programs"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 32, "paragraph_text": "Child care centers and schools can use tested programs and teaching materials about sun safety. The National Cancer Institute's Evidence-Based Cancer Control Programs website lists sun-safety programs for schools.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Sun Safety Facts > Sun safety tips for schools > Use proven educational programs > p[32]", "sha256_of_page": "0b039247f1ac0178bfa4069dace664c34497d63945611d81130beeb49f315800", "sha256_of_paragraph": "f69dc1defc61ab0804ded942014d17fd89d326abced95a7d846aaca6bf3936d8", "source_id": "CDC-SUNSAFE", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/skin-cancer/sun-safety/index.html"}
{"char_offset": [5112, 5334], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:50:21Z", "heading_path": ["Sun Safety Facts", "Sun safety tips for employers"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 33, "paragraph_text": "The Occupational Safety and Health Act requires employers to minimize risk of harm to workers. Employers may be required to provide workers' compensation to employees who get skin cancer because of sun exposure on the job.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Sun Safety Facts > Sun safety tips for employers > p[33]", "sha256_of_page": "0b039247f1ac0178bfa4069dace664c34497d63945611d81130beeb49f315800", "sha256_of_paragraph": "56a7ff73ed48b5f5950c040181c735a75cdeb3ae3bab086c8c19e89a392b19a1", "source_id": "CDC-SUNSAFE", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/skin-cancer/sun-safety/index.html"}
{"char_offset": [5335, 5526], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:50:21Z", "heading_path": ["Sun Safety Facts", "Sun safety tips for employers"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 34, "paragraph_text": "Skin cancer can greatly reduce workers’ productivity. Every year, Americans lose more than $100 million in productivity because of restricted activity or absence from work due to skin cancer.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Sun Safety Facts > Sun safety tips for employers > p[34]", "sha256_of_page": "0b039247f1ac0178bfa4069dace664c34497d63945611d81130beeb49f315800", "sha256_of_paragraph": "4b41d676b0d1556b6a1ee8bc865ac6214c2e2835390240082439f17ef87d4f6d", "source_id": "CDC-SUNSAFE", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/skin-cancer/sun-safety/index.html"}
{"char_offset": [5527, 5789], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:50:21Z", "heading_path": ["Sun Safety Facts", "Sun safety tips for employers"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 35, "paragraph_text": "Providing sun protection for outdoor workers helps create a healthy and safe workplace. It can also increase productivity, which saves money. Some of the tips below protect outdoor workers from heat as well as sun exposure. Learn more about sun exposure at work.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Sun Safety Facts > Sun safety tips for employers > p[35]", "sha256_of_page": "0b039247f1ac0178bfa4069dace664c34497d63945611d81130beeb49f315800", "sha256_of_paragraph": "678b1f696c9a98b1b86b1da8aaeebb40f56ff9e5fd5affdbc7089d35deac5e33", "source_id": "CDC-SUNSAFE", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/skin-cancer/sun-safety/index.html"}
{"char_offset": [5790, 5873], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:50:21Z", "heading_path": ["Sun Safety Facts", "Sun safety tips for employers", "Increase sun protection for employees"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 36, "paragraph_text": "Encourage sun safety among your employees and provide sun protection when possible.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Sun Safety Facts > Sun safety tips for employers > Increase sun protection for employees > p[36]", "sha256_of_page": "0b039247f1ac0178bfa4069dace664c34497d63945611d81130beeb49f315800", "sha256_of_paragraph": "be16413cf3f2421d9d6b47f3b34a5d5c7af58c19b35e6ac81a8f7a284c4bebc6", "source_id": "CDC-SUNSAFE", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/skin-cancer/sun-safety/index.html"}
{"char_offset": [5874, 5946], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:50:21Z", "heading_path": ["Sun Safety Facts", "Sun safety tips for employers", "Increase sun protection for employees"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 37, "paragraph_text": "Use tents, shelters, and cooling stations to provide shade at worksites.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Sun Safety Facts > Sun safety tips for employers > Increase sun protection for employees > p[37]", "sha256_of_page": "0b039247f1ac0178bfa4069dace664c34497d63945611d81130beeb49f315800", "sha256_of_paragraph": "1db98b7fbe8e06bdbc3b8d9c117498d18ec0a2392fd0c20ca880f80533e2d502", "source_id": "CDC-SUNSAFE", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/skin-cancer/sun-safety/index.html"}
{"char_offset": [5947, 6039], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:50:21Z", "heading_path": ["Sun Safety Facts", "Sun safety tips for employers", "Increase sun protection for employees"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 38, "paragraph_text": "Schedule breaks in the shade and allow workers to reapply sunscreen throughout their shifts.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Sun Safety Facts > Sun safety tips for employers > Increase sun protection for employees > p[38]", "sha256_of_page": "0b039247f1ac0178bfa4069dace664c34497d63945611d81130beeb49f315800", "sha256_of_paragraph": "7c3060663f8629e94a19ab0b3a2f92b12b95498b449bfab4c8140811952187fa", "source_id": "CDC-SUNSAFE", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/skin-cancer/sun-safety/index.html"}
{"char_offset": [6040, 6245], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:50:21Z", "heading_path": ["Sun Safety Facts", "Sun safety tips for employers", "Increase sun protection for employees"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 39, "paragraph_text": "Create work schedules that minimize worker's exposure to the sun and heat. For example, schedule outdoor tasks like mowing for early morning instead of noon, and rotate workers to reduce their UV exposure.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Sun Safety Facts > Sun safety tips for employers > Increase sun protection for employees > p[39]", "sha256_of_page": "0b039247f1ac0178bfa4069dace664c34497d63945611d81130beeb49f315800", "sha256_of_paragraph": "f7d89f678f9cbdfa9d73e1edc777f9ed5ae916e458561c2e452d2771962a569f", "source_id": "CDC-SUNSAFE", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/skin-cancer/sun-safety/index.html"}
{"char_offset": [6246, 6436], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:50:21Z", "heading_path": ["Sun Safety Facts", "Sun safety tips for employers", "Add sun safety to workplace policies and training"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 40, "paragraph_text": "Include sun-safety information in workplace wellness programs. The National Cancer Institute's Evidence-Based Cancer Control Programs website lists sun safety programs for outdoor worksites.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Sun Safety Facts > Sun safety tips for employers > Add sun safety to workplace policies and training > p[40]", "sha256_of_page": "0b039247f1ac0178bfa4069dace664c34497d63945611d81130beeb49f315800", "sha256_of_paragraph": "91eea23e997cd5184532181a6db1cbeaea2876d75c8683bc224af98ed6c2a03b", "source_id": "CDC-SUNSAFE", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/skin-cancer/sun-safety/index.html"}
{"char_offset": [6437, 6531], "fetched_at": "2026-11-20T12:50:21Z", "heading_path": ["Sun Safety Facts", "Sun safety tips for employers", "Add sun safety to workplace policies and training"], "language": "en-us", "page_nature": "live", "paragraph_index": 41, "paragraph_text": "Teach outdoor workers about the risks of exposure to UV rays and the symptoms of overexposure.", "published_or_updated": "", "selector": "Sun Safety Facts > Sun safety tips for employers > Add sun safety to workplace policies and training > p[41]", "sha256_of_page": "0b039247f1ac0178bfa4069dace664c34497d63945611d81130beeb49f315800", "sha256_of_paragraph": "d28e6c6946a29eaf70b90bbdfdc3626fa01e30a6d2b84455a8ae2f48f29c07f9", "source_id": "CDC-SUNSAFE", "tag": "p", "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/skin-cancer/sun-safety/index.html"}
